{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01DASwmqQhd1eMDfDzcYHqSM","type":"tool_result","content":"# ACL Rolling Review Form - Scoring Dimensions\n\n## 1. **Reviewer Confidence**\nScale: 1-5\n- **5**: \"Positive that my evaluation is correct. I read the paper very carefully\"\n- **4**: \"Quite sure. I tried to check the important points carefully\"\n- **3**: \"Pretty sure, but there's a chance I missed something\"\n- **2**: \"Willing to defend my evaluation, but it is fairly likely that I missed some details\"\n- **1**: \"Not my area, or paper is very hard to understand\"\n\n## 2. **Soundness**\nScale: 1-5 (with .5 increments)\n- **5**: \"Excellent: This study is one of the most thorough I have seen\"\n- **4**: \"Strong: This study provides sufficient support for all claims\"\n- **3**: \"Acceptable: This study provides sufficient support for its main claims\"\n- **2**: \"Poor: Some of the main claims are not sufficiently supported\"\n- **1**: \"Major Issues: This study is not yet sufficiently thorough to warrant publication\"\n\n## 3. **Excitement**\nScale: 1-5 (with .5 increments)\n- **5**: \"Highly Exciting: I would recommend this paper to others\"\n- **4**: \"Exciting: I would mention this paper to others\"\n- **3**: \"Interesting: I might mention some points of this paper to others\"\n- **2**: \"Potentially Interesting: this paper does not resonate with me\"\n- **1**: \"Not Exciting: this paper does not resonate with me\"\n\n## 4. **Overall Assessment**\nScale: 1-5 (with .5 increments)\n- **5**: \"Consider for Award\"\n- **4**: \"Conference: I think this paper could be accepted\"\n- **3**: \"Findings: I think this paper could be accepted to Findings\"\n- **2**: \"Resubmit next cycle\"\n- **1**: \"Do not resubmit\"\n\n## 5. **Reproducibility**\nScale: 1-5\n- **5**: \"They could easily reproduce the results\"\n- **4**: \"They could mostly reproduce the results\"\n- **3**: \"They could reproduce the results with some difficulty\"\n- **2**: \"They would be hard pressed to reproduce the results\"\n- **1**: \"They would not be able to reproduce the results\"\n\n## 6. **Datasets & Software Value**\nScale: 1-5 each\n- **5**: Enabling impact on research direction\n- **4**: Useful for ongoing work\n- **3**: Potentially useful\n- **2**: Documentary value\n- **1**: No usable contributions\n\n## 7. **Author Identity Knowledge**\nScale: 1-5\n- **5**: Known from double-blind violations\n- **4**: Known from preprints/workshops\n- **3**: Identifiable from submission contents\n- **2**: Known from external sources\n- **1**: No educated guess possible"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"806625a1-4285-4bea-b47f-2ce42ec65c53","uuid":"0b8637da-28ec-4e79-a194-bccaf65f5e50","timestamp":"2026-05-16T10:06:24.537Z","tool_use_result":{"bytes":25365,"code":200,"codeText":"OK","result":"# ACL Rolling Review Form - Scoring Dimensions\n\n## 1. **Reviewer Confidence**\nScale: 1-5\n- **5**: \"Positive that my evaluation is correct. I read the paper very carefully\"\n- **4**: \"Quite sure. I tried to check the important points carefully\"\n- **3**: \"Pretty sure, but there's a chance I missed something\"\n- **2**: \"Willing to defend my evaluation, but it is fairly likely that I missed some details\"\n- **1**: \"Not my area, or paper is very hard to understand\"\n\n## 2. **Soundness**\nScale: 1-5 (with .5 increments)\n- **5**: \"Excellent: This study is one of the most thorough I have seen\"\n- **4**: \"Strong: This study provides sufficient support for all claims\"\n- **3**: \"Acceptable: This study provides sufficient support for its main claims\"\n- **2**: \"Poor: Some of the main claims are not sufficiently supported\"\n- **1**: \"Major Issues: This study is not yet sufficiently thorough to warrant publication\"\n\n## 3. **Excitement**\nScale: 1-5 (with .5 increments)\n- **5**: \"Highly Exciting: I would recommend this paper to others\"\n- **4**: \"Exciting: I would mention this paper to others\"\n- **3**: \"Interesting: I might mention some points of this paper to others\"\n- **2**: \"Potentially Interesting: this paper does not resonate with me\"\n- **1**: \"Not Exciting: this paper does not resonate with me\"\n\n## 4. **Overall Assessment**\nScale: 1-5 (with .5 increments)\n- **5**: \"Consider for Award\"\n- **4**: \"Conference: I think this paper could be accepted\"\n- **3**: \"Findings: I think this paper could be accepted to Findings\"\n- **2**: \"Resubmit next cycle\"\n- **1**: \"Do not resubmit\"\n\n## 5. **Reproducibility**\nScale: 1-5\n- **5**: \"They could easily reproduce the results\"\n- **4**: \"They could mostly reproduce the results\"\n- **3**: \"They could reproduce the results with some difficulty\"\n- **2**: \"They would be hard pressed to reproduce the results\"\n- **1**: \"They would not be able to reproduce the results\"\n\n## 6. **Datasets & Software Value**\nScale: 1-5 each\n- **5**: Enabling impact on research direction\n- **4**: Useful for ongoing work\n- **3**: Potentially useful\n- **2**: Documentary value\n- **1**: No usable contributions\n\n## 7. **Author Identity Knowledge**\nScale: 1-5\n- **5**: Known from double-blind violations\n- **4**: Known from preprints/workshops\n- **3**: Identifiable from submission contents\n- **2**: Known from external sources\n- **1**: No educated guess possible","durationMs":7112,"url":"http://aclrollingreview.org/reviewform"}}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_011qBLGwViLrNygDKPrNPBoo","type":"tool_result","content":"# ARR Reviewer Guidelines Summary\n\n## Core Evaluation Criteria\n\nReviewers assess papers across three main dimensions:\n\n**Soundness**: Technical validity and methodological rigor. Reviews must justify low soundness scores with specific evidence from the paper itself.\n\n**Excitement**: Personal interest and relevance, separate from soundness. \"Low excitement scores without sufficient justification\" indicate your review doesn't accurately reflect your recommendation.\n\n**Overall Recommendation**: A composite assessment combining soundness, excitement, novelty, and potential impact. Papers for main conferences must be \"sufficiently sound and reproducible,\" though you may recommend acceptance even without personal excitement.\n\n## What Reviewers Should Evaluate\n\n**Technical aspects**: Methodology soundness, proof validity, experimental rigor, reproducibility details, and statistical significance.\n\n**Contribution quality**: Clarity of research questions, novelty relative to prior work (published 3+ months before deadline), appropriate scope, and evidence supporting claims.\n\n**Presentation**: Specificity in identifying issues, professional tone, and completeness of related work discussion.\n\n## Common Review Pitfalls to Avoid\n\nReviewers should watch for unconscious biases like dismissing work because \"results seem obvious in retrospect\" or demanding comparisons to closed-source models without clear justification. The guidelines flag 17 specific heuristics that may reflect bias rather than genuine weaknesses.\n\n## Key Responsibilities\n\n- Read papers twice: skim-reading to adjust expectations, then in-depth evaluation\n- Provide specific, actionable feedback rather than vague criticism\n- Acknowledge author responses during discussion periods\n- Flag ethical concerns appropriately rather than using ethics as rejection grounds\n- Maintain strict confidentiality of submissions"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"806625a1-4285-4bea-b47f-2ce42ec65c53","uuid":"b4a63d69-99bd-4861-8310-d79506230982","timestamp":"2026-05-16T10:06:25.146Z","tool_use_result":{"bytes":100603,"code":200,"codeText":"OK","result":"# ARR Reviewer Guidelines Summary\n\n## Core Evaluation Criteria\n\nReviewers assess papers across three main dimensions:\n\n**Soundness**: Technical validity and methodological rigor. Reviews must justify low soundness scores with specific evidence from the paper itself.\n\n**Excitement**: Personal interest and relevance, separate from soundness. \"Low excitement scores without sufficient justification\" indicate your review doesn't accurately reflect your recommendation.\n\n**Overall Recommendation**: A composite assessment combining soundness, excitement, novelty, and potential impact. Papers for main conferences must be \"sufficiently sound and reproducible,\" though you may recommend acceptance even without personal excitement.\n\n## What Reviewers Should Evaluate\n\n**Technical aspects**: Methodology soundness, proof validity, experimental rigor, reproducibility details, and statistical significance.\n\n**Contribution quality**: Clarity of research questions, novelty relative to prior work (published 3+ months before deadline), appropriate scope, and evidence supporting claims.\n\n**Presentation**: Specificity in identifying issues, professional tone, and completeness of related work discussion.\n\n## Common Review Pitfalls to Avoid\n\nReviewers should watch for unconscious biases like dismissing work because \"results seem obvious in retrospect\" or demanding comparisons to closed-source models without clear justification. The guidelines flag 17 specific heuristics that may reflect bias rather than genuine weaknesses.\n\n## Key Responsibilities\n\n- Read papers twice: skim-reading to adjust expectations, then in-depth evaluation\n- Provide specific, actionable feedback rather than vague criticism\n- Acknowledge author responses during discussion periods\n- Flag ethical concerns appropriately rather than using ethics as rejection grounds\n- Maintain strict confidentiality of submissions","durationMs":6729,"url":"http://aclrollingreview.org/reviewerguidelines"}}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_016uxXLv8kiFUJ9xue6iKjsY","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Now I have the complete ARR review form with scoring scales. Let me now proceed with the full dream review process. I need to:\n\n1. Dispatch two reviewers (R1: methods + experiments, R2: novelty + related work)\n2. Run a full roundtable (Step 1: independent reviews → Step 2: cross-sharing → Step 3: convergence → Step 4: synthesis)\n\nLet me now launch both reviewers in parallel. Each will do an independent review, then I'll conduct the roundtable.\n\nLet me organize my understanding of the paper first:\n\n\nThe paper argues that test-time scaling through multi-sample generation and selection fails for structured information extraction tasks like NER and relation extraction, even though confidence signals are actually reliable quality estimators. The authors identify two key failure modes—identical outputs appearing frequently and compressed log-probability ranges—that stem from a deeper \"capability trap\" where stronger models converge on the same outputs, leaving little room for selection. They validate this across multiple NER datasets.\n\nNow I'm checking the experimental coverage against the imagined experiments to verify the paper's claims hold up across different seeds, model sizes, and LoRA configurations.\n\nI'll set up two parallel reviewers to score the paper on soundness, excitement, reproducibility, and confidence using a 1-5 scale with half-point increments.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2344,"cache_read_input_tokens":56777,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":2344},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"806625a1-4285-4bea-b47f-2ce42ec65c53","uuid":"abbf11e7-bee9-40f3-a423-9a9b16f5e504"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_016uxXLv8kiFUJ9xue6iKjsY","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"I have the paper, review context, and ARR scoring rubric. Now dispatching two independent reviewers in parallel (R1: methods + experiments; R2: novelty + related work)."}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2344,"cache_read_input_tokens":56777,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":2344},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"806625a1-4285-4bea-b47f-2ce42ec65c53","uuid":"29650f9d-8380-41c4-a240-a526486b6167"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_016uxXLv8kiFUJ9xue6iKjsY","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_015GqB64KFqf8Hak3CP21zMo","name":"Agent","input":{"description":"R1: Methods + Experiments review","prompt":"You are Reviewer 1 (R1) for a blind peer review of an EMNLP 2026 submission. Your focus is **methods and experiments**.\n\n## ARR Scoring Rubric (EMNLP 2026 uses ACL Rolling Review)\n- **Soundness** (1–5, 0.5 steps): 5=Excellent (one of the most thorough), 4=Strong (sufficient support for all claims), 3=Acceptable (sufficient for main claims), 2=Poor (main claims not sufficiently supported), 1=Major Issues\n- **Excitement** (1–5, 0.5 steps): 5=Highly Exciting (recommend to others), 4=Exciting (mention to others), 3=Interesting, 2=Potentially Interesting, 1=Not Exciting\n- **Overall Assessment** (1–5, 0.5 steps): 5=Award, 4=Conference accept, 3=Findings, 2=Resubmit next cycle, 1=Do not resubmit\n- **Reproducibility** (1–5): 5=Easily reproduce, 4=Mostly reproduce, 3=With some difficulty, 2=Hard pressed, 1=Cannot reproduce\n- **Confidence** (1–5): 5=Positive, 4=Quite sure, 3=Pretty sure, 2=Fairly likely missed details, 1=Not my area\n\n## Paper: \"Strong Predictors, Weak Selectors: Why Confidence Signals Cannot Scale Structured Information Extraction\"\n\n### Abstract\nTest-time scaling via multi-sample generation improves LLM performance on reasoning tasks, but its effectiveness for structured information extraction (IE) remains unclear. They systematically evaluate five confidence signals across four families for quality estimation and best-of-N selection on three NER datasets (CoNLL-2003, SciERC, Few-NERD) and one RE dataset (SciERC), using Qwen3-8B, Qwen3-4B, and LLaMA-3.1-8B. While confidence signals reliably predict output quality (AUROC up to 0.82, Spearman ρ up to 0.45), they universally fail to select better candidates than greedy (T=0) decoding. Two failure mechanisms: (1) instance degeneracy—21–58% of instances produce identical outputs across all N samples; (2) logprob compression—within-instance score ranges of 0.028–0.041 nats. These are unified by a \"capability trap\": higher capability → more convergence → higher degeneracy → less selection headroom. Validated across 3 NER datasets (R²=0.94), two model scales, and a LoRA rank ablation.\n\n### Methods (§3)\n- Problem formulation: Given text x and fine-tuned LLM M, generate N=8 candidate structured outputs at T>0. Evaluate two tasks: Quality Estimation (QE) and Selection.\n- Five signals: Set Jaccard (SJ), F-K Overlap (FK), Exact Match (EM), Voting Confidence (VC), Generation Logprob (LP). SJ uses type-grouped Hungarian matching. LP is the only per-sample signal.\n- Metrics: Spearman ρ, Kendall τ, AUROC (binarized at median F1), ECE for QE. F1 of selected candidate vs. greedy/random/oracle for selection.\n- Statistical significance: bootstrap resampling (1000 iterations), Fisher's combined p-value across seeds.\n\n### Experimental Setup (§4)\n- Datasets: CoNLL-2003 (4 entity types, high capability), SciERC (6 NER types + 7 RE types, moderate difficulty, 43-48% zero-F1 on RE), Few-NERD INTRA (8 coarse types, 5385 instances, 4812 after filtering).\n- Primary model: Qwen3-8B, LoRA rank 16 (α=32), 3 epochs. JSON constrained decoding.\n- Scale ablation: Qwen3-4B with identical LoRA config.\n- Cross-architecture: LLaMA-3.1-8B on CoNLL.\n- LoRA rank ablation: rank 8 on SciERC.\n- N=8 samples at T=1.0 + 1 greedy. 3 seeds (42, 123, 789) for SciERC; 3 seeds for Few-NERD. 1024-token context.\n\n### Results (§5)\n**5.1 QE**: Structural signals (VC, SJ, EM) > logprob across NER datasets. SciERC NER: VC ρ=0.379, SJ ρ=0.363, LP ρ=0.203. Few-NERD: VC ρ=0.43, SJ ρ=0.41, LP ρ=0.28. Fisher's p=0.004 for SJ>LP. On RE, ranking reverses (LP>SJ) due to binary dominance (43-45% zero-F1).\n\n**5.2 Selection**: No signal outperforms greedy on any dataset. SciERC NER: best LP F1=0.654 vs greedy 0.663 (p=0.39). Few-NERD: LP 0.840 vs greedy 0.842 (p=0.72). Fisher combined p=0.42 for greedy≥all. Oracle headroom: +13pp SciERC, +6.6pp Few-NERD, +4.2pp CoNLL.\n\n**5.3 Degeneracy**: 21% (SciERC) to 58% (CoNLL). Non-degenerate subset: LP +2.9pp (p=0.01), within-instance ρ rises to 0.39.\n\n**5.4 LP Compression**: Median LP range 0.041 nats (SciERC), 0.028 nats (Few-NERD). Free-form ablation: std ratio 0.97, confirming compression is intrinsic. Entity-token LP: 4× more variance but worse selection (-2.0pp).\n\n**5.5 Capability trap**: R²=0.94 linear fit of F1 vs degeneracy across 3 datasets. Scale ablation: 8B 21% vs 4B 10.4% degeneracy. LoRA ablation: rank 16 21% vs rank 8 19% (2pp delta vs 10.6pp for scale).\n\n**5.6 Robustness**: LLaMA SJ ρ=0.431 ≈ Qwen 0.436. 3-seed SD ≤ 0.012. CI narrows ~30% with 3rd seed.\n\n### Tables\n- Table 1: QE results (ρ and AUROC across 3 NER datasets). CoNLL column is sparse (only SJ, FK, EM AUROC shown).\n- Table 2: Selection F1 (N=8). CoNLL column sparse (only oracle/greedy shown).\n- Table 3: Capability trap (F1, degeneracy, headroom, best Δ for 3 datasets).\n- Table 4: LoRA rank ablation (rank 8 vs 16 on SciERC).\n- Table 5: 3-seed QE consistency (SciERC).\n- Table 6: CI narrowing from 2→3 seeds.\n\n### Imagined experiments coverage check\nThe review context lists these planned experiments:\n1. Few-NERD smoke test (exp-020) — mentioned briefly as \"200-instance pilot study\"\n2. Few-NERD full (exp-021) — fully reported in Tables 1-3\n3. 3rd seed SciERC (exp-022) — reported in Tables 5-6\n4. LoRA rank=8 ablation (exp-023) — reported in Table 4\n5. Claim audit (exp-024) — the paper appears internally consistent\n\nAll 5 imagined experiments are reflected in the paper.\n\n## Your Task\nWrite a detailed review focusing on:\n1. **Methodological soundness**: Are the five signals well-defined? Is the evaluation protocol rigorous? Are the statistical tests appropriate?\n2. **Experimental design**: Dataset coverage, model coverage, ablation design, seed analysis, sample size adequacy.\n3. **Claims vs evidence**: Does every claim have sufficient experimental support? Are there overclaims?\n4. **Specific weaknesses** in methods/experiments.\n5. **Questions for authors**.\n\nEnd with your independent scores for all 5 ARR dimensions. Be rigorous but fair."},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2344,"cache_read_input_tokens":56777,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":2344},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"806625a1-4285-4bea-b47f-2ce42ec65c53","uuid":"99d43af9-015d-4fff-b366-27ad7936ad28"}
