bility → more convergence → higher degeneracy → less selection headroom. Validated across 3 NER datasets (R²=0.94), two model scales, and a LoRA rank ablation.\n\n### Methods (§3)\n- Problem formulation: Given text x and fine-tuned LLM M, generate N=8 candidate structured outputs at T>0. Evaluate two tasks: Quality Estimation (QE) and Selection.\n- Five signals: Set Jaccard (SJ), F-K Overlap (FK), Exact Match (EM), Voting Confidence (VC), Generation Logprob (LP). SJ uses type-grouped Hungarian matching. LP is the only per-sample signal.\n- Metrics: Spearman ρ, Kendall τ, AUROC (binarized at median F1), ECE for QE. F1 of selected candidate vs. greedy/random/oracle for selection.\n- Statistical significance: bootstrap resampling (1000 iterations), Fisher's combined p-value across seeds.\n\n### Experimental Setup (§4)\n- Datasets: CoNLL-2003 (4 entity types, high capability), SciERC (6 NER types + 7 RE types, moderate difficulty, 43-48% zero-F1 on RE), Few-NERD INTRA (8 coarse types, 5385 instances, 4812 after filtering).\n- Primary model: Qwen3-8B, LoRA rank 16 (α=32), 3 epochs. JSON constrained decoding.\n- Scale ablation: Qwen3-4B with identical LoRA config.\n- Cross-architecture: LLaMA-3.1-8B on CoNLL.\n- LoRA rank ablation: rank 8 on SciERC.\n- N=8 samples at T=1.0 + 1 greedy. 3 seeds (42, 123, 789) for SciERC; 3 seeds for Few-NERD. 1024-token context.\n\n### Results (§5)\n**5.1 QE**: Structural signals (VC, SJ, EM) > logprob across NER datasets. SciERC NER: VC ρ=0.379, SJ ρ=0.363, LP ρ=0.203. Few-NERD: VC ρ=0.43, SJ ρ=0.41, LP ρ=0.28. Fisher's p=0.004 for SJ>LP. On RE, ranking reverses (LP>SJ) due to binary dominance (43-45% zero-F1).\n\n**5.2 Selection**: No signal outperforms greedy on any dataset. SciERC NER: best LP F1=0.654 vs greedy 0.663 (p=0.39). Few-NERD: LP 0.840 vs greedy 0.842 (p=0.72). Fisher combined p=0.42 for greedy≥all. Oracle headroom: +13pp SciERC, +6.6pp Few-NERD, +4.2pp CoNLL.\n\n**5.3 Degeneracy**: 21% (SciERC) to 58% (CoNLL). Non-degenerate