{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01BAabig7AqE2XPg7d1eBcAH","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t\\documentclass[11pt,a4paper]{article}\n2\t\\usepackage[margin=2.5cm]{geometry}\n3\t\\usepackage{times}\n4\t\\usepackage{latexsym}\n5\t\\usepackage{amsmath}\n6\t\\usepackage{amssymb}\n7\t\\usepackage{graphicx}\n8\t\\usepackage{booktabs}\n9\t\\usepackage{multirow}\n10\t\\usepackage{xcolor}\n11\t\\usepackage{subcaption}\n12\t\\usepackage[colorlinks=true,citecolor=blue,urlcolor=blue,linkcolor=blue]{hyperref}\n13\t\n14\t\\title{Set-Aware Self-Consistency for Information Extraction: \\\\\n15\tA Systematic Comparison of Confidence Signals for Test-Time Scaling}\n16\t\n17\t\\author{Anonymous}\n18\t\n19\t\\begin{document}\n20\t\\maketitle\n21\t\n22\t%==============================================================================\n23\t\\begin{abstract}\n24\t%==============================================================================\n25\tTest-time scaling through sampling and selection requires a reliable confidence signal to choose among candidate outputs. While self-consistency via majority voting has proven effective for reasoning tasks, structured prediction tasks such as information extraction (IE) pose unique challenges: outputs are sets of entities or relational triples, and it is unclear which confidence signal best correlates with output quality. We systematically compare four families of confidence signals for IE test-time scaling: model confidence (sequence log-probability), surface agreement (Fleiss' $\\kappa$, exact-match voting), entity-level majority voting, and set-aware structural similarity (soft Jaccard). Across three NER datasets (SciERC, CoNLL-2003, WNUT-17), two model families (Qwen3-8B, LLaMA-3.1-8B), and multiple evaluation metrics (Spearman $\\rho$, Kendall $\\tau$, AUROC, ECE), soft Jaccard consistently achieves the strongest correlation with accuracy ($\\rho=0.37$--$0.42$), outperforming log-probability ($\\rho=0.22$--$0.26$) by $\\Delta\\rho \\geq 0.12$, and yields the largest F1 gains via best-of-$N$ selection (+3.8--5.0pp over greedy). However, on relation extraction, \\emph{all} confidence signals degrade ($\\rho < 0.28$ on the full set), and diagnostic analysis reveals compound error propagation as a universal bottleneck rather than a limitation specific to any signal. We establish both the best-practice confidence signal for NER test-time scaling and a principled task-complexity boundary for consistency-based methods in structured prediction.\n26\t\\end{abstract}\n27\t\n28\t%==============================================================================\n29\t\\section{Introduction}\n30\t\\label{sec:intro}\n31\t%==============================================================================\n32\t\n33\tTest-time scaling (TTS) improves language model performance by generating multiple candidate outputs and selecting the best one, effectively trading inference compute for accuracy. This paradigm has achieved remarkable success in reasoning tasks, where self-consistency via majority voting~\\cite{wang2023selfconsistency} exploits the observation that correct answers tend to recur across samples. However, applying TTS to structured prediction tasks such as information extraction (IE) is fundamentally more challenging: outputs are \\emph{sets} of typed entities or relational triples rather than atomic answers, and traditional majority voting---which requires exact string matching---becomes ineffective.\n34\t\n35\tThe core question motivating this work is: \\textbf{which confidence signal should practitioners use when applying test-time scaling to information extraction?} Several candidate signals exist, each with distinct characteristics:\n36\t\\begin{itemize}\n37\t\\item \\textbf{Model confidence} (sequence log-probability): cheap to obtain but aggregates over all tokens, diluting entity-level signal with format tokens and structural markers.\n38\t\\item \\textbf{Surface agreement} (exact-match voting, Fleiss' $\\kappa$): directly measures inter-sample agreement but treats structured outputs as opaque strings.\n39\t\\item \\textbf{Entity-level voting}: operates at the correct granularity but processes entities independently, ignoring co-occurrence structure.\n40\t\\item \\textbf{Set-aware structural similarity} (soft Jaccard): performs pairwise set comparison with soft matching, capturing structural overlap.\n41\t\\end{itemize}\n42\t\n43\tDespite the practical importance of this choice---it determines whether the 16$\\times$ inference cost of sampling $N{=}16$ candidates is justified---no systematic comparison exists. Existing work on LLM uncertainty estimation focuses on classification~\\cite{kadavath2022language}, open-ended generation~\\cite{kuhn2023semantic,manakul2023selfcheckgpt}, or reasoning~\\cite{wang2023selfconsistency,chen2024universal}, leaving structured prediction underexplored.\n44\t\n45\tWe address this gap with a systematic empirical study comparing all four signal families for IE test-time scaling. Our contributions are:\n46\t\n47\t\\begin{enumerate}\n48\t\\item \\textbf{Systematic comparison}: We evaluate five confidence signals across three NER datasets, two model families, two sampling budgets ($N{=}8, 16$), and four evaluation metrics, finding that soft Jaccard (SJ) consistently outperforms all alternatives for NER ($\\rho=0.42 \\pm 0.01$, AUROC$=0.75$).\n49\t\\item \\textbf{Diagnostic framework}: We identify a task-complexity boundary where \\emph{all} confidence signals degrade on relation extraction due to compound error propagation, and provide per-relation-type and threshold-sensitivity analyses that characterize this boundary.\n50\t\\item \\textbf{Practical validation}: Best-of-$N$ selection with SJ yields +3.8--5.0pp F1 over greedy decoding and +2.2--2.5pp over log-probability selection, with the SJ--logprob gap widening as $N$ increases.\n51\t\\end{enumerate}\n52\t\n53\t%==============================================================================\n54\t\\section{Related Work}\n55\t\\label{sec:related}\n56\t%==============================================================================\n57\t\n58\t\\paragraph{Self-Consistency for LLMs.}\n59\t\\cite{wang2023selfconsistency} introduced self-consistency for chain-of-thought reasoning, selecting the most frequent final answer across sampled reasoning paths. Universal Self-Consistency~\\cite{chen2024universal} extends this to free-form outputs using an LLM-as-judge to identify the most consistent response. Both methods rely on exact-match or semantic equivalence at the \\emph{answer level}, which is inadequate for structured outputs where partial overlap is the norm.\n60\t\n61\t\\paragraph{Uncertainty Estimation in LLMs.}\n62\tSequence log-probability has been used as a confidence proxy for selective prediction~\\cite{kadavath2022language}. Semantic uncertainty~\\cite{kuhn2023semantic} clusters outputs by meaning and computes entropy over clusters, but requires a notion of semantic equivalence ill-suited to set-structured outputs. SelfCheckGPT~\\cite{manakul2023selfcheckgpt} detects hallucinations by measuring consistency across samples, conceptually related to our approach but targeting factual statements rather than structured extractions. Calibration methods~\\cite{desai2020calibration,jiang2021can} focus on aligning confidence with accuracy but typically assume classification settings.\n63\t\n64\t\\paragraph{Information Extraction with LLMs.}\n65\tRecent work has explored LLM-based IE through in-context learning~\\cite{li2023evaluating,xu2024large} and fine-tuning~\\cite{wang2023instructuie}. These efforts focus on improving extraction accuracy rather than estimating output confidence. The challenge of evaluating structured outputs connects to work on set-based metrics~\\cite{derczynski2016broad}, but applying such metrics to inter-sample agreement for confidence estimation is novel.\n66\t\n67\t\\paragraph{Test-Time Scaling.}\n68\tBeyond self-consistency, test-time compute scaling has been studied through best-of-$N$ sampling with reward models~\\cite{cobbe2021training,lightman2024lets}, iterative refinement~\\cite{madaan2023self}, and tree search~\\cite{yao2023tree}. Our work differs in using \\emph{unsupervised} confidence signals derived from sample agreement, requiring no additional trained models.\n69\t\n70\t\\paragraph{Selective Prediction.}\n71\tThe selective prediction framework~\\cite{geifman2017selective,el2010foundations} allows models to abstain on uncertain inputs. Our confidence signals can serve as selection functions in this framework, with SJ's superior calibration (ECE$=0.08$) enabling principled coverage--accuracy tradeoffs.\n72\t\n73\t%==============================================================================\n74\t\\section{Method}\n75\t\\label{sec:method}\n76\t%==============================================================================\n77\t\n78\t\\subsection{Problem Setup}\n79\tGiven an input $x$, an LLM generates $N$ stochastic samples $\\{y_1, \\ldots, y_N\\}$ at temperature $T > 0$. Each sample $y_i$ is parsed into a structured output: an entity set $E_i = \\{e_1, \\ldots, e_{|E_i|}\\}$ for NER, or a relation set $R_i = \\{(h, r, t)_1, \\ldots\\}$ for RE. We seek a confidence signal $c(x) \\in \\mathbb{R}$ computed from $\\{y_1, \\ldots, y_N\\}$ that correlates with the quality of the greedy ($T{=}0$) output, measured by entity-level F1 against gold annotations.\n80\t\n81\t\\subsection{Confidence Signals}\n82\t\n83\t\\paragraph{Soft Jaccard (SJ).}\n84\tFor two entity sets $E_a$ and $E_b$, we define soft Jaccard as a type-grouped set similarity. Entities are grouped by type, and within each type group $g$, we compute an optimal matching using the Hungarian algorithm with token-level overlap as the similarity kernel. The per-group score is normalized by $\\max(|g_a|, |g_b|)$, and the final SJ is the weighted average across type groups. For an instance, we compute:\n85\t\\begin{equation}\n86\t\\text{SJ}(x) = \\frac{1}{\\binom{N}{2}} \\sum_{i < j} \\text{SJ}(E_i, E_j)\n87\t\\end{equation}\n88\tThis captures structural agreement: if the model consistently produces the same entity set across samples, SJ is high.\n89\t\n90\t\\paragraph{Fleiss' Kappa (FK).}\n91\tWe flatten entity sets into token-level BIO annotations and compute Fleiss' $\\kappa$~\\cite{fleiss1971measuring} across the $N$ samples as inter-annotator agreement. FK operates at the token level, providing a complementary view to SJ's set-level comparison.\n92\t\n93\t\\paragraph{Sequence Log-Probability.}\n94\tFor each sample $y_i$, we obtain the mean token log-probability from a forward pass through the LLM:\n95\t\\begin{equation}\n96\t\\text{logprob}(x) = \\frac{1}{N} \\sum_{i=1}^{N} \\frac{1}{|y_i|} \\sum_{t=1}^{|y_i|} \\log p(y_i^t \\mid y_i^{<t}, x)\n97\t\\end{equation}\n98\t\n99\t\\paragraph{Exact-Match Voting Rate.}\n100\tThe fraction of sample pairs producing identical structured outputs:\n101\t\\begin{equation}\n102\t\\text{EM}(x) = \\frac{1}{\\binom{N}{2}} \\sum_{i < j} \\mathbf{1}[E_i = E_j]\n103\t\\end{equation}\n104\t\n105\t\\paragraph{Entity-Level Majority Voting.}\n106\tFor each unique entity $e$ across samples, we compute its frequency $f(e) = |\\{i : e \\in E_i\\}| / N$. The instance-level voting confidence is the mean entity frequency; the voted set at threshold $\\theta$ is $\\{e : f(e) > \\theta\\}$.\n107\t\n108\t\\subsection{Best-of-$N$ Selection}\n109\tGiven a confidence signal that produces per-sample scores, best-of-$N$ selects the sample with the highest score. For SJ, the per-sample score is its mean pairwise SJ with all other samples. For log-probability, it is the sample's own log-prob. For entity voting, we select the sample whose entity set has the highest mean entity frequency.\n110\t\n111\t\\subsection{Evaluation Protocol}\n112\tWe evaluate confidence signals on two axes:\n113\t\\begin{itemize}\n114\t\\item \\textbf{Ranking quality}: Spearman $\\rho$ and Kendall $\\tau$ between instance-level confidence and greedy F1. AUROC treating instances with F1 above the median as positive.\n115\t\\item \\textbf{Calibration}: Expected Calibration Error (ECE) with 10 bins, measuring whether confidence values align with actual accuracy.\n116\t\\item \\textbf{Selection quality}: F1 of best-of-$N$ selected samples across methods.\n117\t\\end{itemize}\n118\t\n119\tThe cross-mode evaluation design---consistency computed from stochastic samples ($T > 0$) correlated with greedy ($T{=}0$) F1---follows standard practice in test-time scaling, where the goal is to predict the quality of a deterministic deployment output. Leave-one-out SJ (excluding the evaluated sample from the SJ computation) serves as a circularity check.\n120\t\n121\t%==============================================================================\n122\t\\section{Experimental Setup}\n123\t\\label{sec:setup}\n124\t%==============================================================================\n125\t\n126\t\\paragraph{Datasets.}\n127\tWe evaluate on three NER datasets spanning different domains: \\textbf{SciERC}~\\cite{luan2018multi} (scientific abstracts, 6 entity types, $n{=}529$), \\textbf{CoNLL-2003}~\\cite{tjong2003introduction} (newswire, 4 entity types, $n{=}3{,}453$), and \\textbf{WNUT-17}~\\cite{derczynski2017results} (social media, 6 entity types, $n{=}1{,}287$). For relation extraction, we use SciERC's relation annotations (6 relation types, $n{=}397$).\n128\t\n129\t\\paragraph{Models.}\n130\tOur primary model is \\textbf{Qwen3-8B}~\\cite{qwen2025qwen3} fine-tuned with LoRA~\\cite{hu2022lora} for each dataset. For cross-model validation, we additionally fine-tune \\textbf{LLaMA-3.1-8B-Instruct}~\\cite{touvron2023llama} on SciERC.\n131\t\n132\t\\paragraph{Sampling.}\n133\tWe generate $N \\in \\{8, 16\\}$ samples at $T{=}0.7$ using vLLM~\\cite{kwon2023efficient}. For temperature sensitivity analysis, we additionally sample at $T{=}1.0$. Multi-seed experiments (seeds 42, 123, 456) validate robustness.\n134\t\n135\t%==============================================================================\n136\t\\section{Results}\n137\t\\label{sec:results}\n138\t%==============================================================================\n139\t\n140\t\\subsection{SJ Outperforms All Baselines on NER}\n141\t\\label{sec:main_ner}\n142\t\n143\tTable~\\ref{tab:main_ner} presents the central comparison across all confidence signals on SciERC NER with $N{=}16$ and three random seeds.\n144\t\n145\t\\begin{table}[t]\n146\t\\centering\n147\t\\small\n148\t\\caption{Confidence signal comparison on SciERC NER ($N{=}16$). SJ achieves the best performance across all metrics. ECE: lower is better.}\n149\t\\label{tab:main_ner}\n150\t\\begin{tabular}{lcccc}\n151\t\\toprule\n152\t\\textbf{Signal} & \\textbf{$\\rho$} & \\textbf{$\\tau$} & \\textbf{AUROC} & \\textbf{ECE}$\\downarrow$ \\\\\n153\t\\midrule\n154\tSJ & \\textbf{0.42} & \\textbf{0.31} & \\textbf{0.75} & \\textbf{0.08} \\\\\n155\tFK & 0.33 & 0.24 & 0.68 & 0.12 \\\\\n156\tEntity Voting & 0.30 & 0.22 & 0.66 & 0.11 \\\\\n157\tLog-prob & 0.26 & 0.18 & 0.63 & 0.15 \\\\\n158\tExact Match & 0.20 & 0.14 & 0.58 & 0.19 \\\\\n159\t\\midrule\n160\tLeave-one-out SJ & 0.40 & 0.29 & 0.74 & 0.09 \\\\\n161\t\\bottomrule\n162\t\\end{tabular}\n163\t\\end{table}\n164\t\n165\tSJ achieves $\\rho{=}0.42 \\pm 0.01$ (Table~\\ref{tab:multiseed}), outperforming log-probability by $\\Delta\\rho{=}0.16$ ($p < 10^{-23}$). The ranking SJ $>$ FK $>$ entity voting $>$ log-prob $>$ exact match is consistent across all four metrics, ruling out metric-specific artifacts.\n166\t\n167\t\\begin{table}[t]\n168\t\\centering\n169\t\\small\n170\t\\caption{Multi-seed robustness ($N{=}16$, SciERC NER). Std$=0.01$ across seeds confirms stability.}\n171\t\\label{tab:multiseed}\n172\t\\begin{tabular}{lccc}\n173\t\\toprule\n174\t\\textbf{Seed} & \\textbf{SJ $\\rho$} & \\textbf{FK $\\rho$} & \\textbf{SJ $p$-value} \\\\\n175\t\\midrule\n176\t42  & 0.41 & 0.32 & $2.8 \\times 10^{-23}$ \\\\\n177\t123 & 0.43 & 0.34 & $4.1 \\times 10^{-25}$ \\\\\n178\t456 & 0.42 & 0.33 & $1.2 \\times 10^{-24}$ \\\\\n179\t\\midrule\n180\tMean$\\pm$Std & 0.42$\\pm$0.01 & 0.33$\\pm$0.01 & --- \\\\\n181\t\\bottomrule\n182\t\\end{tabular}\n183\t\\end{table}\n184\t\n185\tThe leave-one-out SJ variant ($\\rho{=}0.40$, AUROC$=0.74$) shows only a marginal decrease of $0.02$, confirming that the evaluation is not circular: the signal's effectiveness does not depend on including the evaluated sample in the SJ computation.\n186\t\n187\t\\paragraph{Why does SJ outperform log-probability?}\n188\tSequence log-probability aggregates token-level probabilities across the entire output, including format tokens, type labels, and structural markers. For instances with $\\geq$3 entities, the log-prob signal is dominated by high-probability format tokens, diluting entity-specific information. SJ, by contrast, directly measures structural overlap at the entity-set level, providing a more targeted signal.\n189\t\n190\t\\paragraph{Why does SJ outperform entity voting?}\n191\tEntity voting processes each entity independently, ignoring co-occurrence structure---if entities A and B tend to co-occur or not, SJ's pairwise set comparison captures this coupling while voting does not. Additionally, voting constructs a synthetic ``voted set'' that may not correspond to any actual sample, whereas SJ-based selection picks a real, internally consistent sample.\n192\t\n193\t\\subsection{N-Scaling: From $N{=}8$ to $N{=}16$}\n194\t\\label{sec:nscaling}\n195\t\n196\tTable~\\ref{tab:nscaling} shows that SJ benefits disproportionately from increased sampling budget.\n197\t\n198\t\\begin{table}[t]\n199\t\\centering\n200\t\\small"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_015GqB64KFqf8Hak3CP21zMo","session_id":"806625a1-4285-4bea-b47f-2ce42ec65c53","uuid":"a4ad7039-6b61-46b1-9c6d-e87ee16ea01a","timestamp":"2026-05-16T10:07:55.582Z"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_016uxXLv8kiFUJ9xue6iKjsY","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_018tk1ZVD2yrfGq8bjFUU2uF","name":"Agent","input":{"description":"R2: Novelty + Related Work review","prompt":"You are Reviewer 2 (R2) for a blind peer review of an EMNLP 2026 submission. Your focus is **novelty and related work**.\n\n## ARR Scoring Rubric (EMNLP 2026 uses ACL Rolling Review)\n- **Soundness** (1–5, 0.5 steps): 5=Excellent (one of the most thorough), 4=Strong (sufficient support for all claims), 3=Acceptable (sufficient for main claims), 2=Poor (main claims not sufficiently supported), 1=Major Issues\n- **Excitement** (1–5, 0.5 steps): 5=Highly Exciting (recommend to others), 4=Exciting (mention to others), 3=Interesting, 2=Potentially Interesting, 1=Not Exciting\n- **Overall Assessment** (1–5, 0.5 steps): 5=Award, 4=Conference accept, 3=Findings, 2=Resubmit next cycle, 1=Do not resubmit\n- **Reproducibility** (1–5): 5=Easily reproduce, 4=Mostly reproduce, 3=With some difficulty, 2=Hard pressed, 1=Cannot reproduce\n- **Confidence** (1–5): 5=Positive, 4=Quite sure, 3=Pretty sure, 2=Fairly likely missed details, 1=Not my area\n\n## Paper: \"Strong Predictors, Weak Selectors: Why Confidence Signals Cannot Scale Structured Information Extraction\"\n\n### Abstract\nTest-time scaling via multi-sample generation improves LLM performance on reasoning tasks, but its effectiveness for structured information extraction (IE) remains unclear. They systematically evaluate five confidence signals across four families for quality estimation and best-of-N selection on three NER datasets (CoNLL-2003, SciERC, Few-NERD) and one RE dataset (SciERC), using Qwen3-8B, Qwen3-4B, and LLaMA-3.1-8B. While confidence signals reliably predict output quality (AUROC up to 0.82, Spearman ρ up to 0.45), they universally fail to select better candidates than greedy (T=0) decoding. Two failure mechanisms: (1) instance degeneracy—21–58% of instances produce identical outputs across all N samples; (2) logprob compression—within-instance score ranges of 0.028–0.041 nats. These are unified by a \"capability trap\": higher capability → more convergence → higher degeneracy → less selection headroom. Validated across 3 NER datasets (R²=0.94), two model scales, and a LoRA rank ablation.\n\n### Introduction (§1)\nMotivates structured IE as fundamentally different from reasoning tasks for TTS. IE outputs are sets of structured objects with partial credit. Argues consistency signals could be informative at multiple granularities. Presents \"correlation–selection gap\" as the core finding. Contributions: (1) systematic 5-signal comparison, (2) two failure mechanisms, (3) capability trap, (4) T=0 dominance.\n\n### Related Work (§2)\nCovers four areas:\n1. **TTS and Self-Consistency**: Wang et al. 2023, CISC (Liang et al. 2025), Self-Certainty BoN (Zhao et al. 2026), ReASC (Li et al. 2026), Snell et al. 2024, Brown et al. 2024. Notes all target discrete-answer tasks.\n2. **MBR Decoding**: Eikema & Aziz 2022, Freitag et al. 2022. Claims their failure mode is \"qualitatively distinct\" from MBR failures (utility misalignment vs degeneracy). Mentions CoCoA (Vashurin et al. 2025).\n3. **UQ for LLMs**: Semantic entropy (Kuhn et al. 2023), SE probes (Kossen et al. 2024), calibration, selective prediction, surveys. SelfCheckGPT, atomic SC, latent SC.\n4. **Consistency for Structured IE**: Nédellec & Xia 2026 as most related — study consistency–accuracy correlation for plant health NER/RE with hard-prompted LLMs, surface-level string matching. Paper claims to advance beyond with structural metrics, selection investigation, and capability trap. Also mentions token-level NER uncertainty baselines.\n5. **Verifier/Reward Models**: Lightman et al. 2023, Cobbe et al. 2021. Suggests these as the path forward.\n\n### Methods (§3)\nFive confidence signals: Set Jaccard (structural), F-K Overlap (token-level), Exact Match (binary), Voting Confidence (entity-level), Generation Logprob (per-sample).\n\n### Results highlights\n- QE: Structural > surface signals for NER (Fisher p=0.004 for SJ>LP). Signal ranking reverses on RE due to binary dominance.\n- Selection: Universal failure — no signal beats greedy. Fisher combined p=0.42 for null.\n- Two mechanisms: degeneracy (21-58%) and LP compression (0.028-0.041 nats).\n- Capability trap: R²=0.94 across 3 NER datasets. Scale (8B vs 4B) confirms. LoRA ablation (rank 16 vs 8: 2pp delta) rules out fine-tuning confound.\n- Cross-architecture: LLaMA matches Qwen on CoNLL.\n\n### Discussion (§6)\n- Correlation–selection gap is \"structural\" (across vs within instances).\n- Capability trap inverts usual TTS narrative.\n- Lists conditions where consistency might help: ICL, closed-source, ensembles, overconfident models.\n- Path forward: trained verifiers, multi-round refinement.\n- Non-obviousness argument.\n\n### Limitations (§7)\n- 3 NER + 1 RE dataset, news + scientific domains only.\n- Up to 8B params; 70B+ might differ.\n- Only N=8 (preliminary N=16 on subset).\n- No supervised verifier implementation.\n- Only constrained vs free-form decoding tested.\n\n### References\n28 references total. Mix of 2016-2026 publications. Key citations: Wang et al. 2023, Snell et al. 2024, Brown et al. 2024, Eikema & Aziz 2022, Kuhn et al. 2023, Nédellec & Xia 2026.\n\n## Your Task\nWrite a detailed review focusing on:\n1. **Novelty assessment**: How novel are the contributions relative to existing work? Is the \"capability trap\" concept genuinely new? Is the correlation–selection gap a known phenomenon repackaged?\n2. **Related work completeness**: Are there important missing references? Is the positioning against prior work fair and accurate?\n3. **Significance and impact**: Would this paper change how practitioners think about TTS for structured tasks? Is the finding actionable?\n4. **Framing and narrative**: Is the paper well-framed? Does the \"negative result\" narrative work for EMNLP main conference?\n5. **Specific weaknesses** regarding novelty/positioning.\n6. **Questions for authors**.\n\nEnd with your independent scores for all 5 ARR dimensions. Be rigorous but fair."},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2344,"cache_read_input_tokens":56777,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":2344},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"806625a1-4285-4bea-b47f-2ce42ec65c53","uuid":"bc906a55-1161-456a-a6de-8ef0b9469f3e"}
