{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01J5wQVG6vxXpscP3r16EksW","type":"tool_result","content":"200\t\\small\n201\t\\caption{Effect of sampling budget on signal quality (SciERC NER).}\n202\t\\label{tab:nscaling}\n203\t\\begin{tabular}{lcccc}\n204\t\\toprule\n205\t\\textbf{$N$} & \\textbf{SJ $\\rho$} & \\textbf{FK $\\rho$} & \\textbf{Logprob $\\rho$} & \\textbf{Oracle F1} \\\\\n206\t\\midrule\n207\t8  & 0.388 & 0.321 & 0.24 & 0.759 \\\\\n208\t16 & 0.420 & 0.330 & 0.26 & 0.797 \\\\\n209\t\\midrule\n210\t$\\Delta$ & +0.032 & +0.009 & +0.02 & +0.038 \\\\\n211\t\\bottomrule\n212\t\\end{tabular}\n213\t\\end{table}\n214\t\n215\tSJ's improvement (+0.032) is the largest among all signals because pairwise comparisons grow from $\\binom{8}{2}{=}28$ to $\\binom{16}{2}{=}120$, reducing estimation variance by approximately $4\\times$. Oracle F1 increases from 0.759 to 0.797, confirming that more samples produce better candidates.\n216\t\n217\t\\subsection{Cross-Dataset Generalization}\n218\t\\label{sec:crossdataset}\n219\t\n220\tTable~\\ref{tab:crossdataset} demonstrates that the signal ranking generalizes across three NER datasets spanning scientific (SciERC), news (CoNLL-2003), and social media (WNUT-17) domains.\n221\t\n222\t\\begin{table}[t]\n223\t\\centering\n224\t\\small\n225\t\\caption{Cross-dataset comparison ($N{=}8$, $T{=}0.7$, Qwen3-8B). The ranking SJ $>$ FK $>$ EV $>$ Logprob $>$ EM is preserved across all domains.}\n226\t\\label{tab:crossdataset}\n227\t\\begin{tabular}{lccccc}\n228\t\\toprule\n229\t\\textbf{Dataset} & \\textbf{SJ} & \\textbf{FK} & \\textbf{Logprob} & \\textbf{EM} & \\textbf{EV} \\\\\n230\t\\midrule\n231\tSciERC ($n{=}529$) & 0.388 & 0.321 & 0.24 & 0.19 & 0.29 \\\\\n232\tCoNLL ($n{=}3453$) & 0.41 & 0.31 & 0.25 & 0.21 & 0.30 \\\\\n233\tWNUT ($n{=}1287$) & 0.37 & 0.28 & 0.22 & 0.17 & 0.27 \\\\\n234\t\\bottomrule\n235\t\\end{tabular}\n236\t\\end{table}\n237\t\n238\tWNUT-17 yields the lowest SJ $\\rho$ (0.37) due to noisy social media text and emerging entity types, yet SJ still outperforms log-prob by $\\Delta\\rho{=}0.15$. CoNLL-2003's larger test set ($n{=}3{,}453$) provides stronger statistical power ($p{=}4.2 \\times 10^{-38}$).\n239\t\n240\t\\subsection{Cross-Model Generalization}\n241\t\\label{sec:crossmodel}\n242\t\n243\t\\begin{table}[t]\n244\t\\centering\n245\t\\small\n246\t\\caption{Cross-model comparison (SciERC NER, $N{=}8$, $T{=}0.7$).}\n247\t\\label{tab:crossmodel}\n248\t\\begin{tabular}{lcccc}\n249\t\\toprule\n250\t\\textbf{Model} & \\textbf{Greedy F1} & \\textbf{SJ $\\rho$} & \\textbf{FK $\\rho$} & \\textbf{Logprob $\\rho$} \\\\\n251\t\\midrule\n252\tQwen3-8B & 0.662 & 0.388 & 0.321 & 0.24 \\\\\n253\tLLaMA-3.1-8B & 0.629 & 0.38 & 0.30 & 0.23 \\\\\n254\t\\bottomrule\n255\t\\end{tabular}\n256\t\\end{table}\n257\t\n258\tTable~\\ref{tab:crossmodel} shows that LLaMA-3.1-8B reproduces the signal ranking with SJ $\\rho{=}0.38$ and $\\Delta\\rho{=}0.15$ over log-prob. Despite LLaMA's lower base performance (greedy F1$=$0.629 vs 0.662), SJ remains the strongest signal, confirming model-agnostic effectiveness.\n259\t\n260\t\\subsection{Best-of-$N$ Selection}\n261\t\\label{sec:bestofn}\n262\t\n263\tTable~\\ref{tab:bestofn} translates confidence ranking into practical F1 improvements via best-of-$N$ selection ($N{=}16$).\n264\t\n265\t\\begin{table}[t]\n266\t\\centering\n267\t\\small\n268\t\\caption{Best-of-$N$ selection F1 across datasets and methods ($N{=}16$).}\n269\t\\label{tab:bestofn}\n270\t\\begin{tabular}{lccccc}\n271\t\\toprule\n272\t\\textbf{Dataset} & \\textbf{Greedy} & \\textbf{Logprob} & \\textbf{FK} & \\textbf{SJ} & \\textbf{Oracle} \\\\\n273\t\\midrule\n274\tSciERC NER  & 0.662 & 0.687 & 0.698 & \\textbf{0.712} & 0.797 \\\\\n275\tCoNLL NER   & 0.718 & 0.734 & 0.744 & \\textbf{0.756} & 0.815 \\\\\n276\tWNUT NER    & 0.541 & 0.563 & 0.573 & \\textbf{0.586} & 0.695 \\\\\n277\tSciERC RE   & 0.422 & 0.433 & 0.431 & \\textbf{0.436} & 0.553 \\\\\n278\t\\bottomrule\n279\t\\end{tabular}\n280\t\\end{table}\n281\t\n282\t\\begin{table}[t]\n283\t\\centering\n284\t\\small\n285\t\\caption{Pairwise F1 gains (percentage points) from best-of-$N$ selection.}\n286\t\\label{tab:f1gains}\n287\t\\begin{tabular}{lcccc}\n288\t\\toprule\n289\t\\textbf{Dataset} & \\textbf{SJ$-$Greedy} & \\textbf{SJ$-$Logprob} & \\textbf{SJ$-$FK} & \\textbf{Logprob$-$Greedy} \\\\\n290\t\\midrule\n291\tSciERC NER & +5.0 & +2.5 & +1.4 & +2.5 \\\\\n292\tCoNLL NER  & +3.8 & +2.2 & +1.2 & +1.6 \\\\\n293\tWNUT NER   & +4.5 & +2.3 & +1.3 & +2.2 \\\\\n294\tSciERC RE  & +1.4 & +0.3 & +0.5 & +1.1 \\\\\n295\t\\bottomrule\n296\t\\end{tabular}\n297\t\\end{table}\n298\t\n299\tSJ selection yields +3.8--5.0pp over greedy and +2.2--2.5pp over log-prob across all NER datasets. The SJ--logprob advantage justifies the 16$\\times$ inference cost: once the compute budget for sampling is committed, using SJ instead of log-prob for selection adds only $O(N^2)$ CPU computation (less than 1\\% of inference cost) but provides substantial additional F1.\n300\t\n301\t\\paragraph{Selection curves.}\n302\tTable~\\ref{tab:selcurve} shows the SJ--logprob gap widens as $N$ increases, from 1.2pp at $N{=}2$ to 2.5pp at $N{=}16$. SJ's set-level comparison becomes qualitatively richer with more pairwise information, whereas log-prob scores are independent across samples.\n303\t\n304\t\\begin{table}[t]\n305\t\\centering\n306\t\\small\n307\t\\caption{Selection curves on SciERC NER: F1 by $N$ and method.}\n308\t\\label{tab:selcurve}\n309\t\\begin{tabular}{lcccccc}\n310\t\\toprule\n311\t\\textbf{$N$} & \\textbf{Random} & \\textbf{Logprob} & \\textbf{FK} & \\textbf{SJ} & \\textbf{Oracle} & \\textbf{SJ$-$LP} \\\\\n312\t\\midrule\n313\t1  & 0.628 & 0.628 & 0.628 & 0.628 & 0.628 & 0.0 \\\\\n314\t2  & 0.640 & 0.648 & 0.651 & 0.660 & 0.680 & +1.2 \\\\\n315\t4  & 0.652 & 0.668 & 0.674 & 0.685 & 0.728 & +1.7 \\\\\n316\t8  & 0.660 & 0.680 & 0.689 & 0.698 & 0.759 & +1.8 \\\\\n317\t16 & 0.668 & 0.687 & 0.698 & 0.712 & 0.797 & +2.5 \\\\\n318\t\\bottomrule\n319\t\\end{tabular}\n320\t\\end{table}\n321\t\n322\t\\subsection{Exact-Match Baseline: Why Soft Matching Is Necessary}\n323\t\\label{sec:exact_match}\n324\t\n325\t\\begin{table}[t]\n326\t\\centering\n327\t\\small\n328\t\\caption{Exact-match voting statistics (SciERC, $N{=}8$). The extremely low EM rates demonstrate that exact-match voting is inadequate for structured outputs.}\n329\t\\label{tab:em}\n330\t\\begin{tabular}{lccccc}\n331\t\\toprule\n332\t\\textbf{Task} & \\textbf{Mean EM} & \\textbf{Med.\\ EM} & \\textbf{\\%Zero} & \\textbf{$\\rho$} \\\\\n333\t\\midrule\n334\tNER & 0.06 & 0.04 & 68.2 & 0.19 \\\\\n335\tRE  & 0.02 & 0.00 & 87.4 & 0.13 \\\\\n336\t\\bottomrule\n337\t\\end{tabular}\n338\t\\end{table}\n339\t\n340\tTable~\\ref{tab:em} shows that exact-match voting produces near-zero agreement rates: the mean EM rate is only 6\\% for NER and 2\\% for RE. With 68.2\\% of NER instances (87.4\\% for RE) having zero exact matches, the signal is too sparse to provide useful ranking ($\\rho{=}0.19$). This quantifies the fundamental mismatch between the original self-consistency framework~\\cite{wang2023selfconsistency}, designed for atomic answers, and structured outputs where even minor span boundary differences (e.g., ``neural network'' vs.\\ ``neural networks'') break exact matching.\n341\t\n342\t\\subsection{Entity Voting: A Competitive but Limited Baseline}\n343\t\\label{sec:entity_voting}\n344\t\n345\t\\begin{table}[t]\n346\t\\centering\n347\t\\small\n348\t\\caption{Entity voting vs.\\ SJ selection (SciERC NER, $N{=}8$).}\n349\t\\label{tab:entity_voting}\n350\t\\begin{tabular}{lccl}\n351\t\\toprule\n352\t\\textbf{Method} & \\textbf{F1} & \\textbf{Conf.\\ $\\rho$} & \\textbf{Note} \\\\\n353\t\\midrule\n354\tGreedy & 0.662 & --- & $T{=}0$ \\\\\n355\tEntity voting ($\\theta{=}0.5$) & 0.694 & 0.29 & \\\\\n356\tEntity voting ($\\theta{=}0.375$) & 0.701 & 0.29 & Optimal $\\theta$ \\\\\n357\tSJ selection & 0.698 & 0.39 & Max-SJ sample \\\\\n358\tOracle & 0.759 & --- & Best of $N{=}8$ \\\\\n359\t\\bottomrule\n360\t\\end{tabular}\n361\t\\end{table}\n362\t\n363\tEntity voting is the strongest non-SJ baseline ($\\rho{=}0.29$, Table~\\ref{tab:entity_voting}). Its voted-set F1 (0.701 at optimal threshold) is comparable to SJ-selected F1 (0.698), but entity voting requires threshold tuning and produces a single output per threshold, whereas SJ provides a continuous confidence score usable for ranking, calibration, and selection simultaneously.\n364\t\n365\t\\subsection{Log-Probability vs.\\ SJ: Complementary Information}\n366\t\\label{sec:logprob}\n367\t\n368\t\\begin{table}[t]\n369\t\\centering\n370\t\\small\n371\t\\caption{Log-prob vs.\\ SJ/FK correlation (SciERC, $N{=}8$).}\n372\t\\label{tab:logprob}\n373\t\\begin{tabular}{llcccc}\n374\t\\toprule\n375\t\\textbf{Task} & \\textbf{Subset} & \\textbf{SJ} & \\textbf{FK} & \\textbf{Logprob} & \\textbf{SJ$-$LP} \\\\\n376\t\\midrule\n377\tNER & Full & 0.388 & 0.321 & 0.24 & +0.148 \\\\\n378\tNER & Cond. & 0.387 & 0.305 & 0.23 & +0.157 \\\\\n379\tRE  & Full & 0.234 & 0.184 & 0.22 & +0.014 \\\\\n380\tRE  & Cond. & 0.372 & 0.253 & 0.30 & +0.072 \\\\\n381\t\\bottomrule\n382\t\\end{tabular}\n383\t\\end{table}\n384\t\n385\tTable~\\ref{tab:logprob} shows that SJ outperforms log-prob by $\\Delta\\rho{=}0.148$ on NER, far exceeding a meaningful-difference threshold ($\\Delta{=}0.08$). On RE (full set), the gap narrows to $\\Delta\\rho{=}0.014$, consistent with compound error degrading both signals. Even on RE conditional (model-competent subset), SJ maintains $\\Delta\\rho{=}0.072$ over log-prob.\n386\t\n387\t\\subsection{Diagnostic Analysis: Relation Extraction}\n388\t\\label{sec:re_diagnostic}\n389\t\n390\t\\begin{table}[t]\n391\t\\centering\n392\t\\small\n393\t\\caption{RE per-relation-type analysis (SciERC, $N{=}8$). All types show degraded $\\rho$ relative to NER.}\n394\t\\label{tab:re_pertype}\n395\t\\begin{tabular}{lcccc}\n396\t\\toprule\n397\t\\textbf{Relation Type} & \\textbf{Freq.} & \\textbf{SJ $\\rho$} & \\textbf{FK $\\rho$} & $n$ \\\\\n398\t\\midrule\n399\tUsed-for & 0.39 & 0.28 & 0.20 & 155 \\\\"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_015GqB64KFqf8Hak3CP21zMo","session_id":"806625a1-4285-4bea-b47f-2ce42ec65c53","uuid":"d9095389-8cef-42e5-9e8b-cd1285806f2b","timestamp":"2026-05-16T10:08:02.737Z"}
