{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01EeAAgFZac1CTShZZEXFVeh","type":"tool_result","content":"150\tFor QE, we report Spearman $\\rho$, Kendall $\\tau$, AUROC (binarized at median F1), and expected calibration error (ECE) across instances.\n151\tFor selection, we report F1 of the selected candidate, compared to greedy ($T{=}0$) decoding, random selection, and oracle (best possible) selection.\n152\tStatistical significance is assessed via bootstrap resampling (1000 iterations) and Fisher's combined $p$-value across seeds.\n153\t\n154\t% ============================================================\n155\t\\section{Experimental Setup}\n156\t\\label{sec:setup}\n157\t% ============================================================\n158\t\n159\t\\subsection{Datasets}\n160\t\n161\t\\paragraph{CoNLL-2003 \\citep{sang2003conll}.} Standard NER benchmark with 4 entity types (PER, ORG, LOC, MISC).\n162\tHigh-capability regime: models achieve $>$90\\% F1.\n163\t\n164\t\\paragraph{SciERC \\citep{luan2018scierc}.} Scientific NER (6 entity types) and RE (7 relation types) from AI paper abstracts.\n165\tModerate-difficulty NER, challenging RE with 43--48\\% zero-F1 rate.\n166\t\n167\t\\paragraph{Few-NERD \\citep{ding2021fewnerd}.} Fine-grained NER with 8 coarse entity types (INTRA split).\n168\tPositioned between CoNLL and SciERC in difficulty with a large training set (60k+ sentences).\n169\t\n170\tWe include a pilot study (200 instances) to validate the pipeline before the full Few-NERD evaluation (5,385 instances, 4,812 after filtering gold-empty sentences).\n171\t\n172\t\\subsection{Models and Training}\n173\t\n174\t\\paragraph{Primary model.} Qwen3-8B, fine-tuned with LoRA (rank 16, $\\alpha{=}32$) for 3 epochs on each dataset.\n175\tJSON-formatted structured output with constrained decoding.\n176\t\n177\t\\paragraph{Scale ablation.} Qwen3-4B with identical LoRA configuration, evaluated on CoNLL and SciERC.\n178\t\n179\t\\paragraph{Cross-architecture.} LLaMA-3.1-8B fine-tuned on CoNLL to verify model-family independence.\n180\t\n181\t\\paragraph{LoRA rank ablation.} Qwen3-8B with LoRA rank 8 on SciERC to disentangle fine-tuning intensity from base model capability.\n182\t\n183\t\\subsection{Inference}\n184\t\n185\tFor each test instance, we generate $N{=}8$ samples at $T{=}1.0$ and one greedy ($T{=}0$) output.\n186\tAll experiments are repeated across 3 random seeds (42, 123, 789) for SciERC; Few-NERD uses 3 seeds for core metrics.\n187\tResults use 1024-token context throughout.\n188\t\n189\t% ============================================================\n190\t\\section{Results}\n191\t\\label{sec:results}\n192\t% ============================================================\n193\t\n194\t\\subsection{Quality Estimation Across Datasets}\n195\t\\label{sec:qe}\n196\t\n197\tTable~\\ref{tab:qe_comparison} presents quality estimation performance across three NER datasets.\n198\tAll five signals achieve meaningful correlation with output quality, with structural signals (VC, SJ, EM) consistently outperforming logprob.\n199\t\n200\tOn SciERC NER, voting confidence achieves the highest $\\rho{=}0.379$, followed by SJ ($\\rho{=}0.360$), while logprob lags substantially ($\\rho{=}0.205$).\n201\tFor AUROC, exact match leads (0.749), reflecting its effectiveness at binary quality discrimination.\n202\tOn Few-NERD, the pattern holds with higher absolute values: VC $\\rho{=}0.43$, SJ $\\rho{=}0.41$, EM $\\rho{=}0.39$, FK $\\rho{=}0.36$, LP $\\rho{=}0.28$.\n203\tEM achieves the best AUROC of 0.80 on Few-NERD.\n204\t\n205\tThe structural $>$ surface ordering is statistically robust.\n206\tThree-seed Fisher's combined $p$-values on SciERC confirm SJ $>$ LP ($p{=}0.004$) and structural $>$ surface ($p{<}0.001$).\n207\tCross-seed standard deviations are small (SJ: SD${=}0.012$, FK: SD${=}0.008$, LP: SD${=}0.009$), with the third seed narrowing confidence intervals by 30\\%.\n208\t\n209\t\\begin{table}[t]\n210\t\\centering\n211\t\\small\n212\t\\caption{Quality estimation on NER (Qwen3-8B, $N{=}8$, $T{=}1.0$). SciERC values are 3-seed means. Best $\\rho$ and AUROC in \\textbf{bold}.}\n213\t\\label{tab:qe_comparison}\n214\t\\begin{tabular}{@{}l cc cc cc@{}}\n215\t\\toprule\n216\t& \\multicolumn{2}{c}{SciERC} & \\multicolumn{2}{c}{Few-NERD} & \\multicolumn{2}{c}{CoNLL} \\\\\n217\t\\cmidrule(lr){2-3} \\cmidrule(lr){4-5} \\cmidrule(lr){6-7}\n218\tSignal & $\\rho$ & AUC & $\\rho$ & AUC & $\\rho$ & AUC \\\\\n219\t\\midrule\n220\tVC    & \\textbf{.379} & .684 & \\textbf{.43} & .77 & -- & -- \\\\\n221\tSJ    & .363 & .692 & .41 & .78 & .436 & -- \\\\\n222\tEM    & .301 & \\textbf{.749} & .39 & \\textbf{.80} & -- & \\textbf{.816} \\\\\n223\tFK    & .259 & .629 & .36 & .72 & .400 & -- \\\\\n224\tLP    & .203 & .578 & .28 & .71 & -- & -- \\\\\n225\t\\bottomrule\n226\t\\end{tabular}\n227\t\\end{table}\n228\t\n229\t\\paragraph{Task-dependent signal ranking.}\n230\tOn SciERC RE, the signal ranking \\emph{reverses}: LP ($\\rho{=}0.302$) $>$ SJ ($\\rho{=}0.265$) $>$ FK ($\\rho{=}0.254$).\n231\tThis reversal is explained by binary dominance: 43--45\\% of RE instances have F1${=}0$, creating a binary signal distribution where LP's coarse generation-confidence signal captures ``complete failure vs.\\ partial success'' more reliably than structural overlap.\n232\tConditioning on non-zero F1 instances eliminates LP's advantage: conditional LP $\\rho{=}0.011$ ($p{=}0.86$, n.s.), confirming that LP's entire RE advantage is a binary classification effect.\n233\t\n234\t\\subsection{Universal Selection Failure}\n235\t\\label{sec:selection}\n236\t\n237\tDespite meaningful QE correlations, \\emph{no signal outperforms greedy decoding} on any dataset or task.\n238\tTable~\\ref{tab:selection} shows selection F1 across all datasets.\n239\t\n240\t\\begin{table}[t]\n241\t\\centering\n242\t\\small\n243\t\\caption{Best-of-$N$ selection F1 ($N{=}8$, Qwen3-8B). $\\Delta$ is percentage points vs.\\ greedy. No signal significantly outperforms greedy on any dataset.}\n244\t\\label{tab:selection}\n245\t\\begin{tabular}{@{}l ccc@{}}\n246\t\\toprule\n247\tMethod & SciERC & Few-NERD & CoNLL \\\\\n248\t\\midrule\n249\tOracle  & .776 & .908 & -- \\\\\n250\tGreedy  & .663 & .842 & .912 \\\\\n251\tLP      & .654 ($-$0.9) & .840 ($-$0.2) & -- \\\\\n252\tSJ      & .651 ($-$1.2) & .839 ($-$0.3) & -- \\\\\n253\tFK      & -- & .838 ($-$0.4) & -- \\\\\n254\tVC      & .634 ($-$2.9) & .836 ($-$0.6) & -- \\\\\n255\tRandom  & .608 ($-$5.5) & .828 ($-$1.4) & -- \\\\\n256\t\\bottomrule\n257\t\\end{tabular}\n258\t\\end{table}\n259\t\n260\tOn SciERC NER, the best signal (LP) achieves F1${=}0.654$, \\emph{below} greedy at 0.663 (bootstrap $p{=}0.39$, 95\\% CI [$-$3.0, $+$4.2]pp, Cohen's $d{=}0.038$).\n261\tOn Few-NERD, the best signal (LP) achieves 0.840 vs.\\ greedy 0.842 ($-$0.2pp, $p{=}0.72$).\n262\tAll selection gaps are statistically non-significant.\n263\tThree-seed Fisher's combined $p{=}0.42$ for the null hypothesis that greedy $\\geq$ all signals on SciERC NER---three independent runs cannot reject the null.\n264\t\n265\tOn SciERC RE, selection performs even worse: greedy (0.404) exceeds all signals, with the best being LP at 0.395.\n266\t\n267\tYet oracle selection reveals substantial latent headroom: +13.0pp on SciERC NER, +6.6pp on Few-NERD, +4.2pp on CoNLL.\n268\tThe information for improved selection \\emph{exists} in the sample set but no unsupervised signal can access it.\n269\t\n270\t\\subsection{Failure Mechanism 1: Instance Degeneracy}\n271\t\\label{sec:degeneracy}\n272\t\n273\tWe define an instance as \\emph{degenerate} when all $N$ samples produce the same F1 score (typically because the model generates identical or equivalent outputs).\n274\tTable~\\ref{tab:trap} shows degeneracy rates across datasets.\n275\t\n276\t\\begin{table}[t]\n277\t\\centering\n278\t\\small\n279\t\\caption{The capability trap across three NER datasets (Qwen3-8B). Higher F1 $\\rightarrow$ higher degeneracy $\\rightarrow$ less selection headroom. $R^2{=}0.94$ for the F1--degeneracy relationship.}\n280\t\\label{tab:trap}\n281\t\\begin{tabular}{@{}l cccc@{}}\n282\t\\toprule\n283\tDataset & F1 & Degen.\\ & Headroom & Best $\\Delta$ \\\\\n284\t\\midrule\n285\tCoNLL   & .912 & 58\\% & 4.2pp & $+$0.1pp \\\\\n286\tFew-NERD & .842 & 45\\% & 6.6pp & $-$0.2pp \\\\\n287\tSciERC  & .663 & 21\\% & 13.0pp & $+$0.6pp \\\\\n288\t\\bottomrule\n289\t\\end{tabular}\n290\t\\end{table}\n291\t\n292\tDegeneracy rates range from 21\\% (SciERC, moderate difficulty) to 58\\% (CoNLL, high capability), with Few-NERD falling precisely in between at 45\\%.\n293\tThis creates a paradox: on easy datasets where the model is most capable, degeneracy eliminates most selection opportunities; on hard datasets, oracle headroom is large (13pp on SciERC) but no signal can exploit it.\n294\t\n295\tWithin the \\emph{non-degenerate} subset of SciERC instances, LP selection achieves $+$2.9pp over greedy ($p{=}0.01$), and within-instance $\\rho$ rises from 0.08 to 0.39.\n296\tThis confirms that selection \\emph{can} work where outputs genuinely vary---but degeneracy ensures this subset is too small to move the aggregate needle.\n297\t\n298\t\\subsection{Failure Mechanism 2: Logprob Compression}\n299\t\\label{sec:compression}\n300\t\n301\tGeneration logprob is the only signal that varies per sample by construction (all others aggregate to a single instance-level score when all samples are identical).\n302\tHowever, LP exhibits severe within-instance score compression: the median within-instance LP range is 0.041 nats on SciERC and 0.028 nats on Few-NERD.\n303\tOn Few-NERD, 52\\% of instances have effectively tied LP scores.\n304\t\n305\t\\paragraph{Free-form decoding ablation.}\n306\tTo test whether LP compression is caused by constrained (JSON-formatted) decoding, we compare LP distributions under constrained vs.\\ free-form decoding on SciERC.\n307\tLP standard deviation is virtually identical: constrained 0.0206 vs.\\ free-form 0.0199 (ratio 0.97).\n308\tThe tied fraction decreases negligibly from 59.7\\% to 57.7\\%.\n309\tFree-form NER selection: $+$0.18pp vs.\\ greedy (negligible), compared to $+$0.59pp under constrained decoding.\n310\tThis ablation confirms that LP compression is intrinsic to the model's confidence distribution, not an artifact of output format constraints.\n311\tThe fine-tuned model achieves 99.95\\% parse rate (4,406/4,408 valid JSON) even without grammar constraints.\n312\t\n313\t\\paragraph{Entity-token logprob.}\n314\tRestricting LP computation to entity-bearing tokens increases within-instance variance by approximately 4$\\times$.\n315\tHowever, this produces \\emph{worse} selection ($-$2.0pp vs.\\ greedy), likely because the increased variance introduces noise that overwhelms the signal.\n316\t\n317\t\\subsection{The Capability Trap}\n318\t\\label{sec:trap}\n319\t\n320\tThe two failure mechanisms are unified by a capability trap: as model capability (measured by greedy F1) increases on a given task, output distributions converge, driving both higher degeneracy and more severe LP compression.\n321\t\n322\t\\paragraph{Cross-dataset evidence.}\n323\tTable~\\ref{tab:trap} shows a monotonic relationship across three NER datasets, with $R^2{=}0.94$ for a linear fit of greedy F1 vs.\\ degeneracy rate.\n324\tLP compression follows the same pattern: median within-instance LP range decreases from 0.041 nats (SciERC, F1${=}0.66$) to 0.028 nats (Few-NERD, F1${=}0.84$), consistent with higher capability producing tighter output distributions.\n325\t\n326\t\\paragraph{Scale ablation.}\n327\tComparing Qwen3-8B and Qwen3-4B on SciERC NER:\n328\tthe 8B model shows 21\\% degeneracy vs.\\ 10.4\\% for 4B, a 10.6pp gap directly attributable to the larger model's higher capability.\n329\tDespite this, QE correlations are maintained or even improved at 4B (SJ $\\rho$: 8B 0.383 vs.\\ 4B 0.422; FK $\\rho$: 8B 0.259 vs.\\ 4B 0.326), confirming that the correlation--selection gap is not a scale artifact.\n330\tSelection at 4B shows the same pattern: all signals perform worse than greedy on SciERC NER (SJ $-$1.49pp, FK $-$1.56pp, LP $-$2.23pp), despite 13pp of oracle headroom.\n331\t\n332\tOn CoNLL, the 4B model achieves greedy F1${=}0.923$ (vs.\\ 8B's 0.912), with oracle headroom of only 1.70pp---so marginal that selection is inherently indistinguishable from greedy regardless of signal quality.\n333\t\n334\t\\paragraph{LoRA rank ablation.}\n335\tTo rule out fine-tuning intensity as a confound, we compare LoRA rank 16 vs.\\ rank 8 on SciERC NER (Table~\\ref{tab:lora}).\n336\tHalving LoRA capacity changes degeneracy by only 2pp (19\\% vs.\\ 21\\%), well within our 5pp equivalence threshold.\n337\tIn contrast, halving base model parameters (8B $\\rightarrow$ 4B) changes degeneracy by 10.6pp.\n338\tThis 2:10.6 ratio confirms that the capability trap is driven by pretrained model capability, not fine-tuning recipe.\n339\t\n340\t\\begin{table}[t]\n341\t\\centering\n342\t\\small\n343\t\\caption{LoRA rank ablation on SciERC NER (Qwen3-8B). Halving adapter capacity has minimal effect ($\\Delta{\\leq}2$pp on degeneracy) while all metrics remain within 5pp equivalence.}\n344\t\\label{tab:lora}\n345\t\\begin{tabular}{@{}l ccc@{}}\n346\t\\toprule\n347\tMetric & Rank 8 & Rank 16 & $\\Delta$ \\\\\n348\t\\midrule\n349\tGreedy F1      & .648 & .663 & $-$1.5pp \\\\"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_015GqB64KFqf8Hak3CP21zMo","session_id":"806625a1-4285-4bea-b47f-2ce42ec65c53","uuid":"43f8d2c6-2527-4c7a-9bce-581baa99ae64","timestamp":"2026-05-16T10:08:15.449Z"}
