{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01WExsPkB2mYj4qda3taeYAY","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_017TGYjDckFftGECeCct4onT","name":"Read","input":{"file_path":"/home/ubuntu/.agent-ml-research-idea_gen_0514_1/projects/struct_self_consist_ie/artifacts/dream_reports/dream_20260516T092241/best/paper.tex","offset":350,"limit":200},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":4732,"cache_read_input_tokens":50064,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":4732,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":34,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_015GqB64KFqf8Hak3CP21zMo","session_id":"806625a1-4285-4bea-b47f-2ce42ec65c53","uuid":"3e8f398f-55a7-4737-8267-169fd88e80cf"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_017TGYjDckFftGECeCct4onT","type":"tool_result","content":"350\tDegeneracy     & 19\\% & 21\\% & $-$2.0pp \\\\\n351\tOracle headroom & 12.4pp & 13.0pp & $-$0.6pp \\\\\n352\tSJ $\\rho$      & .372 & .360 & $+$1.2pp \\\\\n353\tLP $\\rho$      & .195 & .205 & $-$1.0pp \\\\\n354\tBest $-$ greedy & $-$0.5pp & $+$0.6pp & $-$1.1pp \\\\\n355\t\\bottomrule\n356\t\\end{tabular}\n357\t\\end{table}\n358\t\n359\tQE correlations are also stable across ranks: SJ $\\rho$ changes by only 0.012, LP $\\rho$ by 0.010.\n360\tSelection fails at both ranks (best signal $-$0.5pp and $+$0.6pp vs.\\ greedy, both n.s.).\n361\t\n362\t\\subsection{Cross-Model and Robustness Validation}\n363\t\\label{sec:robustness}\n364\t\n365\t\\paragraph{Cross-architecture.}\n366\tLLaMA-3.1-8B on CoNLL NER achieves SJ $\\rho{=}0.431$, closely matching Qwen3-8B's 0.436, demonstrating model-family independence.\n367\t\n368\t\\paragraph{Three-seed consistency.}\n369\tTable~\\ref{tab:3seed} shows that all QE metrics are highly reproducible across seeds on SciERC NER, with standard deviations $\\leq$0.012.\n370\tThe third seed reduces confidence interval widths by approximately 30\\% while maintaining or strengthening all statistical conclusions (Table~\\ref{tab:ci}).\n371\t\n372\t\\begin{table}[t]\n373\t\\centering\n374\t\\small\n375\t\\caption{Three-seed QE consistency on SciERC NER (Qwen3-8B, $N{=}8$). All signals show SD $\\leq$ 0.012.}\n376\t\\label{tab:3seed}\n377\t\\begin{tabular}{@{}l cccccc@{}}\n378\t\\toprule\n379\tSignal & s42 & s123 & s789 & Mean & SD \\\\\n380\t\\midrule\n381\tSJ  & .360 & .362 & .368 & .363 & .012 \\\\\n382\tFK  & .267 & .255 & .258 & .259 & .008 \\\\\n383\tVC  & .379 & .372 & .385 & .379 & .007 \\\\\n384\tEM  & .295 & .308 & .301 & .301 & .007 \\\\\n385\tLP  & .205 & .208 & .198 & .203 & .009 \\\\\n386\t\\bottomrule\n387\t\\end{tabular}\n388\t\\end{table}\n389\t\n390\t\\begin{table}[t]\n391\t\\centering\n392\t\\small\n393\t\\caption{Confidence interval narrowing from 2 to 3 seeds on SciERC NER. All primary claims maintain significance.}\n394\t\\label{tab:ci}\n395\t\\begin{tabular}{@{}l cccc@{}}\n396\t\\toprule\n397\tClaim & 2-seed & 3-seed & $\\downarrow$\\% & Sig? \\\\\n398\t\\midrule\n399\tSJ $>$ LP  & .146 & .102 & 30\\% & $p{=}.004$ \\\\\n400\tSJ $>$ FK  & .128 & .089 & 30\\% & $p{=}.001$ \\\\\n401\tgreedy $\\geq$ all & [${-}$.03,${+}$.04] & [${-}$.02,${+}$.03] & 25\\% & all n.s. \\\\\n402\t\\bottomrule\n403\t\\end{tabular}\n404\t\\end{table}\n405\t\n406\t\\paragraph{Few-NERD seed stability.}\n407\tOn Few-NERD, SJ $\\rho$ across three seeds shows mean 0.409 with SD${=}0.006$, even more stable than SciERC, consistent with the larger test set (4,812 vs.\\ 529 instances).\n408\t\n409\t% ============================================================\n410\t\\section{Discussion}\n411\t\\label{sec:discussion}\n412\t% ============================================================\n413\t\n414\t\\paragraph{The correlation--selection gap is structural.}\n415\tOur results reveal a fundamental asymmetry: confidence signals operate across instances (ranking which inputs are harder), while selection operates within instances (ranking which candidates are better).\n416\tFour of five signals tested---SJ, FK, EM, VC---are instance-level aggregates by construction: when all $N$ samples are identical (the degenerate case), these signals collapse to a constant.\n417\tThis is not a failure of signal design but a definitional property---a methodological premise that the community should recognize when applying self-consistency to structured IE.\n418\t\n419\t\\paragraph{Implications for test-time scaling.}\n420\tThe capability trap implies a sobering conclusion for practitioners: the better a model performs on a structured IE task, the \\emph{less} benefit multi-sample strategies can provide.\n421\tAt greedy F1${=}0.91$ (CoNLL), 58\\% of instances are already degenerate.\n422\tThis inverts the usual TTS narrative, where more capable models are expected to benefit more from scaling inference compute.\n423\tFor structured IE, $T{=}0$ greedy decoding at zero additional cost is the dominant strategy.\n424\t\n425\t\\paragraph{When would consistency help?}\n426\tOur results apply to fine-tuned models with well-calibrated generation.\n427\tConsistency signals may still benefit: (a) in-context learning without fine-tuning, where output distributions are more diverse; (b) closed-source API settings where logprobs are unavailable; (c) ensemble or mixture-of-experts settings; and (d) tasks where the model is systematically overconfident.\n428\tThe non-degenerate subset result ($+$2.9pp, $p{=}0.01$) suggests that any technique reducing output convergence would unlock selection potential.\n429\t\n430\t\\paragraph{Path forward.}\n431\tClosing the capability trap likely requires fundamentally different approaches.\n432\tTrained verifiers \\citep{cobbe2021training,lightman2023lets} can learn task-specific quality predictors that are not limited by output-distribution statistics.\n433\tMulti-round refinement strategies that condition on previous outputs may break the convergence pattern.\n434\tThe oracle headroom (4--13pp across datasets) confirms the information exists; the challenge is accessing it.\n435\t\n436\t\\paragraph{Non-obviousness.}\n437\tOne might argue that selection failure for fine-tuned models is predictable.\n438\tWe counter with three points: (a) self-consistency demonstrably helps for reasoning tasks \\citep{wang2023selfconsistency}, making IE failure non-obvious; (b) structured outputs have internal structure that surface metrics cannot capture, suggesting structural signals might succeed where simple voting fails; and (c) the \\emph{degree} of failure---and the specific mechanisms driving it---are not predictable a priori.\n439\t\n440\t% ============================================================\n441\t\\section{Limitations}\n442\t\\label{sec:limitations}\n443\t% ============================================================\n444\t\n445\tOur analysis covers three NER datasets and one RE dataset from scientific and news domains.\n446\tInformal text domains (social media, clinical notes) are not represented.\n447\tWe evaluate models up to 8B parameters; 70B+ models may exhibit different degeneracy dynamics, though our scale ablation suggests the capability trap would intensify.\n448\tThe free-form ablation tests one alternative to constrained decoding; other decoding strategies (nucleus sampling with varying $p$, top-$k$) are not explored.\n449\tWe do not implement supervised verifiers, which our analysis identifies as a promising direction---this is deliberate scope control for a diagnostic paper.\n450\tAll experiments use $N{=}8$ samples; preliminary evidence on a 200-instance subset suggests $N{=}16$ may improve RE correlations, but this requires full-scale confirmation.\n451\t\n452\t% ============================================================\n453\t\\section{Conclusion}\n454\t\\label{sec:conclusion}\n455\t% ============================================================\n456\t\n457\tWe present a systematic diagnostic analysis of confidence signals for structured information extraction, revealing a fundamental correlation--selection gap.\n458\tFive confidence signals reliably predict output quality across instances (AUROC up to 0.82, $\\rho$ up to 0.45) but universally fail to select better candidates than zero-cost greedy decoding.\n459\tWe diagnose two failure mechanisms---instance degeneracy (21--58\\% of instances produce identical outputs) and logprob compression (median within-instance range 0.028--0.041 nats)---unified by a capability trap: higher model capability drives output convergence, eliminating selection headroom ($R^2{=}0.94$ across three NER datasets).\n460\tScale and LoRA rank ablations confirm this trap is driven by pretrained capability, not fine-tuning intensity.\n461\tOur results establish that for structured IE, test-time scaling via multi-sample selection is dominated by simple greedy decoding, and that closing the gap requires fundamentally different approaches.\n462\t\n463\t% ============================================================\n464\t% References (abbreviated for review)\n465\t% ============================================================\n466\t{\\small\n467\t\\begin{thebibliography}{99}\n468\t\n469\t\\bibitem[Brown et~al.(2024)]{brown2024large}\n470\tBrown, B. et~al. (2024).\n471\tLarge language monkeys: Scaling inference compute with repeated sampling.\n472\t\\textit{arXiv:2407.21787}.\n473\t\n474\t\\bibitem[Chen et~al.(2024)]{chen2024confidence}\n475\tChen, X. et~al. (2024).\n476\tQuantifying uncertainty in natural language generation: A survey of confidence estimation methods.\n477\t\\textit{NAACL 2024}.\n478\t\n479\t\\bibitem[Chen et~al.(2025)]{chen2025latent}\n480\tChen, Z. et~al. (2025).\n481\tLatent self-consistency.\n482\t\\textit{arXiv:2508.18395}.\n483\t\n484\t\\bibitem[Cobbe et~al.(2021)]{cobbe2021training}\n485\tCobbe, K. et~al. (2021).\n486\tTraining verifiers to solve math word problems.\n487\t\\textit{arXiv:2110.14168}.\n488\t\n489\t\\bibitem[Culotta \\& McCallum(2004)]{culotta2004confidence}\n490\tCulotta, A. and McCallum, A. (2004).\n491\tConfidence estimation for information extraction.\n492\t\\textit{HLT-NAACL 2004}.\n493\t\n494\t\\bibitem[Desai \\& Durrett(2020)]{desai2020calibration}\n495\tDesai, S. and Durrett, G. (2020).\n496\tCalibration of pre-trained transformers.\n497\t\\textit{EMNLP 2020}.\n498\t\n499\t\\bibitem[Ding et~al.(2021)]{ding2021fewnerd}\n500\tDing, N. et~al. (2021).\n501\tFew-NERD: A few-shot named entity recognition dataset.\n502\t\\textit{ACL 2021}.\n503\t\n504\t\\bibitem[Eikema \\& Aziz(2022)]{eikema2022sampling}\n505\tEikema, B. and Aziz, W. (2022).\n506\tSampling-based approximations to minimum Bayes risk decoding.\n507\t\\textit{EACL 2022}.\n508\t\n509\t\\bibitem[Freitag et~al.(2022)]{freitag2022high}\n510\tFreitag, M. et~al. (2022).\n511\tHigh quality rather than high model probability.\n512\t\\textit{TACL}.\n513\t\n514\t\\bibitem[Gal \\& Ghahramani(2016)]{gal2016dropout}\n515\tGal, Y. and Ghahramani, Z. (2016).\n516\tDropout as a {B}ayesian approximation.\n517\t\\textit{ICML 2016}.\n518\t\n519\t\\bibitem[Geifman \\& El-Yaniv(2017)]{geifman2017selective}\n520\tGeifman, Y. and El-Yaniv, R. (2017).\n521\tSelective classification for deep neural networks.\n522\t\\textit{NeurIPS 2017}.\n523\t\n524\t\\bibitem[Guo et~al.(2017)]{guo2017calibration}\n525\tGuo, C. et~al. (2017).\n526\tOn calibration of modern neural networks.\n527\t\\textit{ICML 2017}.\n528\t\n529\t\\bibitem[Huang et~al.(2025)]{huang2025uqcalibration}\n530\tHuang, L. et~al. (2025).\n531\tA survey on uncertainty quantification and calibration for LLMs.\n532\t\\textit{arXiv:2503.15850}.\n533\t\n534\t\\bibitem[Kadavath et~al.(2022)]{kadavath2022language}\n535\tKadavath, S. et~al. (2022).\n536\tLanguage models (mostly) know what they know.\n537\t\\textit{arXiv:2207.05221}.\n538\t\n539\t\\bibitem[Kossen et~al.(2024)]{kossen2024semantic}\n540\tKossen, J. et~al. (2024).\n541\tSemantic entropy probes.\n542\t\\textit{arXiv:2406.15927}.\n543\t\n544\t\\bibitem[Kuhn et~al.(2023)]{kuhn2023semantic}\n545\tKuhn, L. et~al. (2023).\n546\tSemantic uncertainty.\n547\t\\textit{ICLR 2023}.\n548\t\n549\t\\bibitem[Lakshminarayanan et~al.(2017)]{lakshminarayanan2017simple}"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_015GqB64KFqf8Hak3CP21zMo","session_id":"806625a1-4285-4bea-b47f-2ce42ec65c53","uuid":"ed32319f-14e6-4b70-b758-ff28beb4c473","timestamp":"2026-05-16T10:08:20.430Z"}
