{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01SfF8uuh2DMu5ejZKA2GcCo","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01DXs5f469cbdb6gkWQTyimc","name":"Read","input":{"file_path":"/home/ubuntu/.agent-ml-research-idea_gen_0514_1/projects/struct_self_consist_ie/artifacts/dream_reports/dream_20260516T092241/best/paper.tex","limit":100},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":968,"cache_read_input_tokens":60085,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":968,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":1,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_018tk1ZVD2yrfGq8bjFUU2uF","session_id":"806625a1-4285-4bea-b47f-2ce42ec65c53","uuid":"41cd619c-3e92-43f5-bfd7-cdbb5d11011d"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01DXs5f469cbdb6gkWQTyimc","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t\\documentclass[11pt,twocolumn]{article}\n2\t\\usepackage[utf8]{inputenc}\n3\t\\usepackage[T1]{fontenc}\n4\t\\usepackage{times}\n5\t\\usepackage{geometry}\n6\t\\geometry{a4paper,margin=2.5cm}\n7\t\\usepackage{booktabs}\n8\t\\usepackage{amsmath,amssymb}\n9\t\\usepackage{graphicx}\n10\t\\usepackage{xcolor}\n11\t\\usepackage{hyperref}\n12\t\\usepackage{multirow}\n13\t\\usepackage{tablefootnote}\n14\t\\usepackage{natbib}\n15\t\\bibliographystyle{plainnat}\n16\t\\usepackage{caption}\n17\t\\captionsetup{font=small}\n18\t\n19\t\\title{Strong Predictors, Weak Selectors:\\\\Why Confidence Signals Cannot Scale\\\\Structured Information Extraction}\n20\t\n21\t\\author{Anonymous EMNLP 2026 Submission}\n22\t\\date{}\n23\t\n24\t\\begin{document}\n25\t\\maketitle\n26\t\n27\t% ============================================================\n28\t\\begin{abstract}\n29\t% ============================================================\n30\tTest-time scaling via multi-sample generation improves LLM performance on reasoning tasks, but its effectiveness for structured information extraction (IE) remains unclear.\n31\tWe systematically evaluate five confidence signals across four families for quality estimation and best-of-$N$ selection on three NER datasets (CoNLL-2003, SciERC, Few-NERD) and one RE dataset (SciERC), using Qwen3-8B, Qwen3-4B, and LLaMA-3.1-8B.\n32\tWhile confidence signals reliably predict output quality (AUROC up to 0.82 on CoNLL, Spearman $\\rho$ up to 0.45), they \\emph{universally fail} to select better candidates than greedy ($T{=}0$) decoding.\n33\tWe diagnose two failure mechanisms: (1)~\\textbf{instance degeneracy}---21--58\\% of instances produce identical outputs across all $N$ samples, eliminating selection headroom; and (2)~\\textbf{logprob compression}---the only per-sample signal exhibits within-instance score ranges of merely 0.028--0.041 nats, rendering candidate discrimination impossible.\n34\tThese mechanisms are unified by a \\textbf{capability trap}: as model capability increases, outputs converge, degeneracy rises, and selection headroom vanishes.\n35\tWe confirm this pattern across three NER datasets ($R^2{=}0.94$ for the F1--degeneracy relationship), two model scales, and a LoRA rank ablation that rules out fine-tuning intensity as a confound.\n36\tOur results establish that $T{=}0$ greedy decoding dominates all multi-sample strategies for structured IE, and that closing the gap requires fundamentally different approaches such as trained verifiers.\n37\t\\end{abstract}\n38\t\n39\t% ============================================================\n40\t\\section{Introduction}\n41\t\\label{sec:intro}\n42\t% ============================================================\n43\t\n44\tTest-time scaling (TTS)---generating multiple candidate outputs and selecting the best one---has emerged as a powerful strategy for improving LLM performance \\citep{wang2023selfconsistency,snell2024scaling,brown2024large}.\n45\tSelf-consistency \\citep{wang2023selfconsistency} and minimum Bayes risk (MBR) decoding \\citep{eikema2022sampling} demonstrate that multi-sample strategies can substantially outperform single-sample greedy decoding on reasoning and translation tasks.\n46\t\n47\tStructured information extraction (IE)---extracting typed entity sets and relational triples from text---presents a fundamentally different challenge.\n48\tUnlike multiple-choice reasoning where answers are discrete tokens, IE outputs are \\emph{sets of structured objects} with partial credit: two candidate entity sets may share some entities but differ on others.\n49\tThis structural richness suggests that consistency-based confidence signals could be particularly informative, as they can measure agreement at multiple granularities (exact match, entity overlap, structural similarity).\n50\t\n51\tWe present a systematic diagnostic analysis of five confidence signals spanning four families---structural consistency (set Jaccard, F-K overlap), exact match agreement, voting confidence, and generation logprob---evaluated for both quality estimation (QE) and best-of-$N$ selection.\n52\tOur analysis reveals a stark \\textbf{correlation--selection gap}: signals that reliably \\emph{rank instances} by quality (Spearman $\\rho$ up to 0.45, AUROC up to 0.82) universally \\emph{fail to select better candidates} from within each instance's sample set.\n53\t\n54\tWe diagnose why this gap exists by identifying two failure mechanisms:\n55\t\\begin{enumerate}\n56\t\\item \\textbf{Instance degeneracy.} A capable fine-tuned model produces identical outputs across all $N{=}8$ samples for 21--58\\% of instances, eliminating any selection headroom. Where outputs do vary, MBR-style selection closes 23--41\\% of the oracle gap---but degeneracy renders the aggregate effect negligible.\n57\t\\item \\textbf{Logprob compression.} Generation logprob---the only signal that varies per sample by construction---exhibits within-instance score ranges of 0.028--0.041 nats (median), too compressed for reliable candidate discrimination. A controlled free-form decoding ablation confirms this compression is intrinsic to the model, not an artifact of constrained decoding (std ratio 0.97).\n58\t\\end{enumerate}\n59\t\n60\tThese mechanisms are unified by a \\textbf{capability trap}: higher model capability $\\rightarrow$ more output convergence $\\rightarrow$ higher degeneracy $\\rightarrow$ less selection headroom.\n61\tWe validate this relationship across three NER datasets spanning a wide difficulty range (CoNLL F1=0.91, Few-NERD F1=0.84, SciERC F1=0.66), finding a strong monotonic relationship between task-specific capability and degeneracy rate ($R^2{=}0.94$).\n62\tScale ablation (8B vs.\\ 4B) and LoRA rank ablation (rank 16 vs.\\ 8) confirm that the trap is driven by pretrained model capability, not fine-tuning intensity.\n63\t\n64\tOur contributions are:\n65\t\\begin{itemize}\n66\t\\item A systematic comparison of five confidence signals for structured IE, establishing that quality estimation and selection are fundamentally different problems (\\S\\ref{sec:qe});\n67\t\\item Diagnosis of two failure mechanisms---instance degeneracy and logprob compression---explaining why selection fails (\\S\\ref{sec:degeneracy}, \\S\\ref{sec:compression});\n68\t\\item The capability trap as a unifying explanation, validated across datasets, model scales, and fine-tuning configurations (\\S\\ref{sec:trap});\n69\t\\item Evidence that $T{=}0$ greedy decoding dominates all multi-sample strategies for structured IE at zero additional inference cost.\n70\t\\end{itemize}\n71\t\n72\t% ============================================================\n73\t\\section{Related Work}\n74\t\\label{sec:related}\n75\t% ============================================================\n76\t\n77\t\\paragraph{Test-Time Scaling and Self-Consistency.}\n78\t\\citet{wang2023selfconsistency} show that sampling multiple chain-of-thought reasoning paths and selecting via majority vote (self-consistency) substantially improves accuracy on reasoning benchmarks.\n79\tSubsequent work extends this paradigm: CISC \\citep{liang2025cisc} uses confidence-weighted majority voting, Self-Certainty Best-of-N \\citep{zhao2026selfcertainty} employs KL-divergence for reward-free selection, and ReASC \\citep{li2026reasc} introduces reliability-aware adaptive consistency.\n80\t\\citet{snell2024scaling} and \\citet{brown2024large} provide theoretical frameworks for TTS compute-optimal allocation.\n81\tAll these methods target tasks with discrete answers (multiple choice, numerical); structured IE outputs---variable-length entity/relation sets with partial credit---are qualitatively different.\n82\t\n83\t\\paragraph{MBR Decoding and Sample Selection.}\n84\tMBR decoding selects the candidate minimizing expected loss under a utility function \\citep{eikema2022sampling,freitag2022high}.\n85\t\\citet{eikema2022sampling} document MBR failures in machine translation due to utility function misalignment.\n86\tOur failure mode is qualitatively distinct: within-instance utility functions \\emph{are} aligned (within-instance $\\rho{=}0.52$), but instance degeneracy eliminates the headroom that MBR requires.\n87\tCoCoA \\citep{vashurin2025cocoa} combines confidence and consistency for UQ in QA/summarization/translation but does not address structured IE.\n88\t\n89\t\\paragraph{Uncertainty Quantification for LLMs.}\n90\tSemantic entropy \\citep{kuhn2023semantic} and semantic entropy probes \\citep{kossen2024semantic} estimate uncertainty via meaning clustering.\n91\tCalibration methods \\citep{guo2017calibration,kadavath2022language,desai2020calibration} and selective prediction \\citep{geifman2017selective,whitehead2025know} provide complementary perspectives.\n92\tRecent surveys \\citep{ye2025uq,huang2025uqcalibration,chen2024confidence} offer comprehensive overviews.\n93\tSelfCheckGPT \\citep{manakul2023selfcheckgpt} detects hallucinations via sampling consistency.\n94\tAtomic self-consistency \\citep{li2024atomic} operates at sub-claim granularity for long-form text, while latent self-consistency \\citep{chen2025latent} works in embedding space.\n95\t\n96\t\\paragraph{Consistency for Structured IE.}\n97\tMost closely related, \\citet{nedellec2026consistency} study consistency--accuracy correlation in hard-prompted LLMs for plant health NER/RE.\n98\tThey use surface-level string matching, which they acknowledge ``overlooks semantically consistent outputs.''\n99\tOur structural metrics (set Jaccard, F-K overlap) directly address this limitation.\n100\tFurthermore, they do not investigate best-of-$N$ selection, multi-signal comparison, or the capability trap."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_018tk1ZVD2yrfGq8bjFUU2uF","session_id":"806625a1-4285-4bea-b47f-2ce42ec65c53","uuid":"f714804f-ee38-47d5-82da-f598b013fa94","timestamp":"2026-05-16T10:08:43.920Z"}
