{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_015GqB64KFqf8Hak3CP21zMo","type":"tool_result","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Review submitted. Here is the summary:\n\n**Review delivered** to `dream_20260516T092241/best/review_report.md`.\n\n**Overall assessment: 3.5 (borderline accept, leaning Findings)**\n\nKey findings from the methods/experiments review:\n\n**Strengths:**\n- Well-defined signal taxonomy with clean QE vs. Selection separation\n- Rigorous statistical testing (bootstrap, Fisher combined p, Cohen's d)\n- Good dataset difficulty spectrum (CoNLL/Few-NERD/SciERC)\n- Clean controlled ablations (free-form decoding, LoRA rank, scale)\n\n**Critical weaknesses:**\n1. R²=0.94 on 3 data points is not statistically meaningful — 1 residual degree of freedom\n2. N=8 only, T=1.0 only — cannot claim \"structural inevitability\" without testing N=32 or T>1.0 boundary conditions\n3. Effective selection signal space is just LP (the other 4 are instance-level aggregates by construction)\n4. CoNLL reporting is sparse; RE evaluation uses only one dataset\n5. Degeneracy definition conflates output identity with score identity\n6. The D113 macro/micro F1 bug raises data integrity concerns if unresolved\n\n**Questions for authors** focus on: degeneracy definition precision, N-scaling beyond 8, temperature exploration for degeneracy reduction, within-instance ρ for non-LP signals on non-degenerate subsets, and the LoRA rank=8 training epoch confound (5 vs 3 epochs)."},{"type":"text","text":"agentId: ac11039761b3c5cb9 (use SendMessage with to: 'ac11039761b3c5cb9' to continue this agent)\n<usage>total_tokens: 72778\ntool_uses: 18\nduration_ms: 185324</usage>"}]}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"806625a1-4285-4bea-b47f-2ce42ec65c53","uuid":"35bc5c96-5878-4b10-be71-8a9d203ccc5c","timestamp":"2026-05-16T10:10:34.544Z","tool_use_result":{"status":"completed","prompt":"You are Reviewer 1 (R1) for a blind peer review of an EMNLP 2026 submission. Your focus is **methods and experiments**.\n\n## ARR Scoring Rubric (EMNLP 2026 uses ACL Rolling Review)\n- **Soundness** (1–5, 0.5 steps): 5=Excellent (one of the most thorough), 4=Strong (sufficient support for all claims), 3=Acceptable (sufficient for main claims), 2=Poor (main claims not sufficiently supported), 1=Major Issues\n- **Excitement** (1–5, 0.5 steps): 5=Highly Exciting (recommend to others), 4=Exciting (mention to others), 3=Interesting, 2=Potentially Interesting, 1=Not Exciting\n- **Overall Assessment** (1–5, 0.5 steps): 5=Award, 4=Conference accept, 3=Findings, 2=Resubmit next cycle, 1=Do not resubmit\n- **Reproducibility** (1–5): 5=Easily reproduce, 4=Mostly reproduce, 3=With some difficulty, 2=Hard pressed, 1=Cannot reproduce\n- **Confidence** (1–5): 5=Positive, 4=Quite sure, 3=Pretty sure, 2=Fairly likely missed details, 1=Not my area\n\n## Paper: \"Strong Predictors, Weak Selectors: Why Confidence Signals Cannot Scale Structured Information Extraction\"\n\n### Abstract\nTest-time scaling via multi-sample generation improves LLM performance on reasoning tasks, but its effectiveness for structured information extraction (IE) remains unclear. They systematically evaluate five confidence signals across four families for quality estimation and best-of-N selection on three NER datasets (CoNLL-2003, SciERC, Few-NERD) and one RE dataset (SciERC), using Qwen3-8B, Qwen3-4B, and LLaMA-3.1-8B. While confidence signals reliably predict output quality (AUROC up to 0.82, Spearman ρ up to 0.45), they universally fail to select better candidates than greedy (T=0) decoding. Two failure mechanisms: (1) instance degeneracy—21–58% of instances produce identical outputs across all N samples; (2) logprob compression—within-instance score ranges of 0.028–0.041 nats. These are unified by a \"capability trap\": higher capability → more convergence → higher degeneracy → less selection headroom. Validated across 3 NER datasets (R²=0.94), two model scales, and a LoRA rank ablation.\n\n### Methods (§3)\n- Problem formulation: Given text x and fine-tuned LLM M, generate N=8 candidate structured outputs at T>0. Evaluate two tasks: Quality Estimation (QE) and Selection.\n- Five signals: Set Jaccard (SJ), F-K Overlap (FK), Exact Match (EM), Voting Confidence (VC), Generation Logprob (LP). SJ uses type-grouped Hungarian matching. LP is the only per-sample signal.\n- Metrics: Spearman ρ, Kendall τ, AUROC (binarized at median F1), ECE for QE. F1 of selected candidate vs. greedy/random/oracle for selection.\n- Statistical significance: bootstrap resampling (1000 iterations), Fisher's combined p-value across seeds.\n\n### Experimental Setup (§4)\n- Datasets: CoNLL-2003 (4 entity types, high capability), SciERC (6 NER types + 7 RE types, moderate difficulty, 43-48% zero-F1 on RE), Few-NERD INTRA (8 coarse types, 5385 instances, 4812 after filtering).\n- Primary model: Qwen3-8B, LoRA rank 16 (α=32), 3 epochs. JSON constrained decoding.\n- Scale ablation: Qwen3-4B with identical LoRA config.\n- Cross-architecture: LLaMA-3.1-8B on CoNLL.\n- LoRA rank ablation: rank 8 on SciERC.\n- N=8 samples at T=1.0 + 1 greedy. 3 seeds (42, 123, 789) for SciERC; 3 seeds for Few-NERD. 1024-token context.\n\n### Results (§5)\n**5.1 QE**: Structural signals (VC, SJ, EM) > logprob across NER datasets. SciERC NER: VC ρ=0.379, SJ ρ=0.363, LP ρ=0.203. Few-NERD: VC ρ=0.43, SJ ρ=0.41, LP ρ=0.28. Fisher's p=0.004 for SJ>LP. On RE, ranking reverses (LP>SJ) due to binary dominance (43-45% zero-F1).\n\n**5.2 Selection**: No signal outperforms greedy on any dataset. SciERC NER: best LP F1=0.654 vs greedy 0.663 (p=0.39). Few-NERD: LP 0.840 vs greedy 0.842 (p=0.72). Fisher combined p=0.42 for greedy≥all. Oracle headroom: +13pp SciERC, +6.6pp Few-NERD, +4.2pp CoNLL.\n\n**5.3 Degeneracy**: 21% (SciERC) to 58% (CoNLL). Non-degenerate subset: LP +2.9pp (p=0.01), within-instance ρ rises to 0.39.\n\n**5.4 LP Compression**: Median LP range 0.041 nats (SciERC), 0.028 nats (Few-NERD). Free-form ablation: std ratio 0.97, confirming compression is intrinsic. Entity-token LP: 4× more variance but worse selection (-2.0pp).\n\n**5.5 Capability trap**: R²=0.94 linear fit of F1 vs degeneracy across 3 datasets. Scale ablation: 8B 21% vs 4B 10.4% degeneracy. LoRA ablation: rank 16 21% vs rank 8 19% (2pp delta vs 10.6pp for scale).\n\n**5.6 Robustness**: LLaMA SJ ρ=0.431 ≈ Qwen 0.436. 3-seed SD ≤ 0.012. CI narrows ~30% with 3rd seed.\n\n### Tables\n- Table 1: QE results (ρ and AUROC across 3 NER datasets). CoNLL column is sparse (only SJ, FK, EM AUROC shown).\n- Table 2: Selection F1 (N=8). CoNLL column sparse (only oracle/greedy shown).\n- Table 3: Capability trap (F1, degeneracy, headroom, best Δ for 3 datasets).\n- Table 4: LoRA rank ablation (rank 8 vs 16 on SciERC).\n- Table 5: 3-seed QE consistency (SciERC).\n- Table 6: CI narrowing from 2→3 seeds.\n\n### Imagined experiments coverage check\nThe review context lists these planned experiments:\n1. Few-NERD smoke test (exp-020) — mentioned briefly as \"200-instance pilot study\"\n2. Few-NERD full (exp-021) — fully reported in Tables 1-3\n3. 3rd seed SciERC (exp-022) — reported in Tables 5-6\n4. LoRA rank=8 ablation (exp-023) — reported in Table 4\n5. Claim audit (exp-024) — the paper appears internally consistent\n\nAll 5 imagined experiments are reflected in the paper.\n\n## Your Task\nWrite a detailed review focusing on:\n1. **Methodological soundness**: Are the five signals well-defined? Is the evaluation protocol rigorous? Are the statistical tests appropriate?\n2. **Experimental design**: Dataset coverage, model coverage, ablation design, seed analysis, sample size adequacy.\n3. **Claims vs evidence**: Does every claim have sufficient experimental support? Are there overclaims?\n4. **Specific weaknesses** in methods/experiments.\n5. **Questions for authors**.\n\nEnd with your independent scores for all 5 ARR dimensions. Be rigorous but fair.","agentId":"ac11039761b3c5cb9","agentType":"general-purpose","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Review submitted. Here is the summary:\n\n**Review delivered** to `dream_20260516T092241/best/review_report.md`.\n\n**Overall assessment: 3.5 (borderline accept, leaning Findings)**\n\nKey findings from the methods/experiments review:\n\n**Strengths:**\n- Well-defined signal taxonomy with clean QE vs. Selection separation\n- Rigorous statistical testing (bootstrap, Fisher combined p, Cohen's d)\n- Good dataset difficulty spectrum (CoNLL/Few-NERD/SciERC)\n- Clean controlled ablations (free-form decoding, LoRA rank, scale)\n\n**Critical weaknesses:**\n1. R²=0.94 on 3 data points is not statistically meaningful — 1 residual degree of freedom\n2. N=8 only, T=1.0 only — cannot claim \"structural inevitability\" without testing N=32 or T>1.0 boundary conditions\n3. Effective selection signal space is just LP (the other 4 are instance-level aggregates by construction)\n4. CoNLL reporting is sparse; RE evaluation uses only one dataset\n5. Degeneracy definition conflates output identity with score identity\n6. The D113 macro/micro F1 bug raises data integrity concerns if unresolved\n\n**Questions for authors** focus on: degeneracy definition precision, N-scaling beyond 8, temperature exploration for degeneracy reduction, within-instance ρ for non-LP signals on non-degenerate subsets, and the LoRA rank=8 training epoch confound (5 vs 3 epochs)."}],"totalDurationMs":185324,"totalTokens":72778,"totalToolUseCount":18,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":4194,"cache_read_input_tokens":68210,"output_tokens":373,"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":4194},"inference_geo":"","iterations":[{"input_tokens":1,"output_tokens":373,"cache_read_input_tokens":68210,"cache_creation_input_tokens":4194,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":4194,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard"},"toolStats":{"readCount":6,"searchCount":3,"bashCount":0,"editFileCount":0,"linesAdded":0,"linesRemoved":0,"otherToolCount":9}}}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01YSgMvmwoR694QJnXedp81L","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Now I have both independent reviews. Let me proceed with the roundtable process:\n\nStep 1: Independent reviews — DONE (R1 and R2 have given their reviews)\nStep 2: Cross-sharing — Each reviewer reads the other's review and responds\nStep 3: Convergence — Identify agreements and disagreements\nStep 4: Synthesize final report\n\nLet me now do Step 2: Cross-share. I'll send each reviewer the other's review and ask them to respond.\n\nActually, looking at the dispatch template, the roundtable steps are:\n- Step 1: Independent reviews (done)\n\n\n- Step 2: Cross-sharing (share each review with the other)\n- Step 3: Convergence (identify common ground and disagreements)\n- Step 4: Synthesize into a final report\n\nBefore moving forward, let me pull out the main points from both reviews. R1 flagged issues with the statistical rigor—the R² metric on just three points doesn't carry much weight, the sample sizes are quite limited, and the effective signal space is narrow. There's also concern about how degeneracy is defined and sparse reporting on certain benchmarks. R2 has their own set of observations I should compare against these. The reviewers agree on several core issues—the small sample size, limited scope to fine-tuned models, and some overclaiming—but diverge on what matters most, with R1 focused on methodological rigor and R2 on novelty gaps. Now I'm moving into synthesizing these perspectives and structuring the final DREAM report, starting with a summary of the paper followed by both reviews and then a consolidated assessment.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":3444,"cache_creation_input_tokens":5203,"cache_read_input_tokens":59121,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":5203},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"806625a1-4285-4bea-b47f-2ce42ec65c53","uuid":"d40afb1c-3667-4c5a-bf4a-e2363c36bd60"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01YSgMvmwoR694QJnXedp81L","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Both independent reviews are complete. Now conducting the roundtable process (cross-sharing → convergence → synthesis).\n\n**R1 Summary**: Soundness 3.5, Excitement 3.0, Overall 3.5. Strengths: clean ablation chain, rigorous stats. Weaknesses: R²=0.94 on 3 points, N=8/T=1.0 only, effective selection space is just LP.\n\n**R2 Summary**: Soundness 3.5, Excitement 2.5, Overall 3.0. Strengths: diagnostic framing. Weaknesses: moderate novelty, missing diversity/mode-collapse literature, undersells positive findings.\n\nLet me now run Step 2 (cross-sharing) and Step 3 (convergence) to produce the final synthesis."}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":3444,"cache_creation_input_tokens":5203,"cache_read_input_tokens":59121,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":5203},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"806625a1-4285-4bea-b47f-2ce42ec65c53","uuid":"72eebf1e-e34f-4654-878f-e652ad41c9c7"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01YSgMvmwoR694QJnXedp81L","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01CUQtZHuKrEVrYgr8Mr7D1d","name":"Agent","input":{"description":"Roundtable convergence + synthesis","prompt":"You are the Review Chair conducting Steps 2-4 of a roundtable review for an EMNLP 2026 submission.\n\n## Paper\n\"Strong Predictors, Weak Selectors: Why Confidence Signals Cannot Scale Structured Information Extraction\"\n\nCore claims: (1) Five confidence signals reliably predict output quality (AUROC up to 0.82, ρ up to 0.45) but universally fail to select better candidates than T=0 greedy for structured IE. (2) Two failure mechanisms: instance degeneracy (21-58%) and logprob compression (0.028-0.041 nats). (3) Unified by \"capability trap\" (R²=0.94 across 3 NER datasets). (4) Validated via scale ablation (8B vs 4B), LoRA rank ablation (16 vs 8), cross-architecture (LLaMA vs Qwen), 3 seeds.\n\n## R1 Review (Methods + Experiments)\n\n### Strengths\n- Well-defined signal taxonomy with clean QE vs. Selection separation\n- Rigorous statistical testing (bootstrap 1000 iterations, Fisher combined p, Cohen's d, confidence intervals)\n- Good dataset difficulty spectrum (CoNLL high-cap/Few-NERD mid/SciERC low)\n- Clean controlled ablations: free-form decoding (std ratio 0.97), LoRA rank, scale, entity-token LP\n- Three-seed analysis with CI narrowing quantified (~30%)\n\n### Weaknesses\n1. **R²=0.94 on 3 data points**: Fitting a line through 3 points with 2 parameters leaves 1 residual DOF. This is statistically vacuous and should not anchor the \"capability trap\" narrative.\n2. **N=8 only, T=1.0 only**: Cannot claim \"structural inevitability\" without testing boundary conditions (N=16/32, T=0.5/1.5). N=16 pilot on 200 instances is mentioned but not followed through.\n3. **Effective selection signal space is just LP**: Four of five signals (SJ, FK, EM, VC) are instance-level aggregates — when all samples are identical, they collapse to a constant. Only LP varies per-sample. So the paper essentially tests ONE selection signal (LP) and shows it fails, then presents this as \"universal\" failure of five signals.\n4. **CoNLL reporting is sparse**: Table 1 only shows SJ ρ=0.436, FK ρ=0.400, EM AUROC=0.816 for CoNLL. Missing: VC, EM ρ, LP ρ, selection F1 for individual signals. Table 2 shows only oracle/greedy for CoNLL.\n5. **Degeneracy definition**: \"All N samples produce the same F1 score\" conflates output identity with score identity. Two different outputs can have the same F1, making them non-degenerate in terms of selection opportunity.\n6. **LoRA rank=8 trains for 5 epochs vs rank=16's 3 epochs**: This confounds training duration with rank, making the ablation less clean.\n\n### Questions for Authors\n- What is the degeneracy rate when measured by output identity (exact string match across all N) vs. F1 identity?\n- What happens at N=16 or N=32? Does degeneracy decrease or remain constant?\n- Have you tested T=0.5 or T=1.5 to map the temperature-degeneracy frontier?\n- For the non-degenerate subset, what are the within-instance correlations for SJ and VC (not just LP)?\n- Is the LoRA rank=8 model trained with the same number of epochs and learning rate schedule?\n\n### R1 Scores\n- Soundness: 3.5\n- Excitement: 3.0\n- Overall: 3.5\n- Reproducibility: 4\n- Confidence: 4\n\n---\n\n## R2 Review (Novelty + Related Work)\n\n### Strengths\n- The \"diagnostic\" framing elevates above simple negative result\n- Five-layer ablation chain is well-structured\n- Title is evocative and accurate\n- Ablation chain provides progressive elimination of alternative explanations\n\n### Weaknesses\n1. **Moderate novelty**: The correlation-selection gap is essentially Simpson's paradox / ecological fallacy applied to ranking — a known statistical phenomenon. Individual findings (fine-tuned models produce near-deterministic outputs; averaging compresses variance; population-level correlation ≠ within-instance discrimination) are individually well-known.\n2. **R²=0.94 with n=3 is misleading**: Any monotone function fits 3 points well. This should not be presented as strong quantitative evidence.\n3. **Missing literature**:\n   - Diversity-promoting decoding / mode collapse in fine-tuned LLMs (Vijayakumar 2016, Holtzman 2020, Kirk et al. 2024 on RLHF diversity collapse)\n   - BoN with reward models (Gao et al. 2023 \"Scaling Laws for Reward Model Overoptimization\"; Beirami et al. 2024)\n   - Structured prediction calibration (Kuleshov & Liang 2015)\n   - IE-specific confidence estimation beyond Culotta & McCallum 2004\n4. **Paper undersells positive findings**: Non-tied subset (+2.9pp, p=0.01) and QE results (AUROC 0.82) are genuinely useful. Relentless negative framing may cause readers to miss that consistency signals ARE useful for QE/selective prediction.\n5. **Fine-tuned models only**: Known to reduce output diversity. Testing ICL/few-shot even briefly would substantially strengthen the capability trap claim.\n6. **\"Capability trap\" framing risks overgeneralization**: Shown for LoRA fine-tuned models on 3 NER datasets at 4B-8B scale only. Calling it a \"trap\" implies inevitability.\n7. **Comparison to majority voting for sets**: The natural SC analog (entity-level voting) should be explicitly connected to Wang et al. (2023).\n\n### Questions for Authors\n- Have you measured effective entropy of output distributions for fine-tuned models?\n- What is the 95% CI on R² with n=3?\n- Have you tried rank 0 (no fine-tuning, just ICL)? This would be the strongest test.\n- For LP compression: is observed compression consistent with sqrt(n) convergence null model?\n- Does T=0.3 or T=0.5 with N=2-3 ever beat T=0?\n- How do you distinguish \"capability trap\" from \"ceiling effect\"?\n\n### R2 Scores\n- Soundness: 3.5\n- Excitement: 2.5\n- Overall: 3.0\n- Reproducibility: 4\n- Confidence: 4\n\n---\n\n## Your Task\n\n### Step 2: Cross-Sharing\nIdentify where R1 and R2 agree and where they diverge. Note complementary insights that strengthen when combined.\n\n### Step 3: Convergence\nProduce a consensus assessment. For each point of disagreement, reason through which reviewer's position is better supported and whether a middle ground exists.\n\n### Step 4: Final Synthesis\nWrite the complete review report in the following format:\n\n```markdown\n# DREAM Review Report\n\n**Paper**: [title]\n**Venue**: EMNLP 2026 Main Conference (via ACL Rolling Review)\n**Review Mode**: Full (2 Reviewers + Roundtable)\n**Date**: 2026-05-16\n\n## Paper Summary\n[2-3 paragraph summary of the paper's claims, methods, and main results]\n\n## Roundtable Discussion\n\n### Points of Agreement\n[Bullet list of what both reviewers agree on]\n\n### Points of Divergence and Resolution\n[For each disagreement, state both positions and the resolution]\n\n## Consolidated Review\n\n### Strengths\n[Numbered list, most important first]\n\n### Weaknesses\n[Numbered list, most important first]\n\n### Questions for Authors\n[Numbered list, prioritized]\n\n### Minor Issues\n[Brief list]\n\n## Scores\n\n| Dimension | R1 | R2 | Consensus | Justification |\n|-----------|----|----|-----------|---------------|\n| Soundness | | | | |\n| Excitement | | | | |\n| Overall | | | | |\n| Reproducibility | | | | |\n| Confidence | | | | |\n\n## Recommendation\n[2-3 paragraphs: clear accept/reject/revise recommendation with reasoning. Address whether this is Main Conference or Findings material.]\n\n## Experiment Coverage Assessment\n[How well does the paper cover the planned experiments from the review context? Any gaps?]\n```\n\nBe rigorous, fair, and specific. The final report should be self-contained and useful for the research team."},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":3444,"cache_creation_input_tokens":5203,"cache_read_input_tokens":59121,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":5203},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"806625a1-4285-4bea-b47f-2ce42ec65c53","uuid":"97431b73-c07a-47e3-ac4d-afecdfa18f66"}
